      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Patent SELF-RISING FLOUR IWZIrioN The Examiner, Friday, March 13, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</p>
        <p>said pora, recalling a quaint, half-ruined" hail cm the moors. "I remember driving to their place one day, and an old woman told me the last of them were dead. Another family lives- in the hall now. I forget the name." "No one knows it but themselves," cried Mrs. Tremewan with a toss of her head; "that new family kept a shop in Bristol or somewhere. I know it was a shop, at all events. But about the Pillage family? Ah, yes, poor Walter was the last of them. He was so handsome and so poor. I would have married him if he had not been so poor," went- on Mrs. Tremewan with a sigh; "he was so clever. George and Francis were both jealous of Walter because I praised him. But he is dead." . "When did he die?" _ _ _</p>
        <p>"He was lost at sea. I met hint in London, and he went to India. The ship was wrecked in the Indian Ocean, they say. I never rightly heard the truth, but I 'do know that Walter was drowned. Your father told me so, and he is never wrong. Poor Walter! Don't speak of him, Dora. It makes My heart ache."</p>
        <p>The -girl did not speak for a moment. She was wondering what the three men spoken of .by Mrs. Tremewan could have seen in such a weak little woman to love her so much. Yet she knew that what her mother said must be true, for Hanna- ford had certainly not spoken to her father for over twenty years, and she saw with her awn eyes and heard with her own ears how her -father idolised his wife. Men were strange ermitures. Mrs. Tremewan never had been distinguished for intellect, and although she was undeniably beauti- ful, yet surely something more than • beauty was needed to attract a man. Dora reflected for a few moments, but could come to no solution of the problem, and finally took refuge from dis- turbing thoughts in caressing James. James was a black Japanese pug, and by no means—so far as his own private opinion went— the least important personage in the Gray Roma. He had a tip-tilted nose, a wrinkled face, and his skin was as black as ink. Also it was loose, which proved his high descent, and he usually went about decorated with a silver collar. Mrs. Tremewan was devoted to James, and was scrupulously careful as to his food, never • allow- ing him scraps or sweets. Consequently James was in the pink of condition and. very good tempered, which last pleasing trait arose from his prysession of a healthy liver. He was devoted to his mis- tress, end on the whole was one of the chief joys of Mrs. Tremewan's life. When Dora caress:d him he took it as a liberty and whimpered for hi s mistress. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Did he then," said Mrs. Tremewan, hastily gathering up her work and throwing it aside for the pug. "Dora, what have you been doing to him?" "Nothing. I 'don't think he likes me," said the girl. . "Yet he is a most offeetionate doe.. What a. strange girl you are. First your father and then James. No one seems to like you." "Mother!" Dora rose to her feet deeply wounded, "how can you spaak so."</p>
        <p>"Speak, how. There, there, James, be quiet. Oh, I see what you mean. I didn't intend to offend you, Dora, .but you really are a queer girl. You are always wanting to be loved. I have had a lot of it, and I assure you it becomes wearisome after a time. If your father gives you plenty of money, and I'm sure he does, you need not trouble about these things. But you are always so queer. James, be quiet. Doi:4't whimper. You have quite upset the poor animal, Dora. I wonder at you!" MTS. Tremewan babbled on and devoted herself to soothing the feelings of the black pug. She cared for these far more than for those of her daughter, who stood beside her tall and severe in her black dress. But, after all, what was the use of making trouble, thought Dora. Her mother could not, or rather would not, understand her feelings. She was a feeble doll, who should have been kept in a glass case all her life. And aq a matter of fact it was just in this way that Mrs. Tremewan had passed her existence. Tremewan would not have let the air of heaven blow too roughly on her if he could have helped. Dora moved to the end of the room, and tried to subdue her feelings. In that house she reflected feelings of any sort were out of place. "I'm sure I look quite a fright," said Mrs. Tremewan, getting up and looking at herself in the mirror, "and there is your father. Give me that screen Dora. I don't wish him to see how flushed I am. Ah, Francis, there you are dear!" It was Tremewan who entered the room in response to this greeting. He looked a fine man in his evening clothes, and there was a softer smile than usual on his face. Yet as he stepped across to kiss the hand his wife held out to him Dora thought that he had an anxious look in his eyes. She therefore watched him more closely than usual, and he soon became aware of her scrutiny. At once he resented it.</p>
        <p>"What are you looking at, Dora?" he said sharply, and rtsing from his knees—he had been fondly kneeling by the sofa of his earthly idol. "You have a most unpleasant habit of staring." Dora -laughed. She could not help it. 'One must look sometimes, father," she said gaily, "and I have beard it said that a cat may look at a king." "Then Francis is a king," cried Mrs. Tre- mewan; "he is my king." Her husband smiled on her, and then frowned on his daughter. "I have had a stupid evening," he said abruptly.; " Gillard is the worst hand at a rubber I ever came across. The new doctor was there."</p>
        <p>"He is very nice, Sue tells me," said. Mrs. Tremewasi.</p>
        <p>"A good-looking ass," growled Tremewan dis- dainfully. "I suppose he will call and you will flirt with him."</p>
        <p>"I am too old to flirt," said Mrs. Treme-waa with a pretty flush. "You are never old," murmured her husband, and Dom, beginning to find this scene somewhat unpleasant, went to the door. Her father called her back. "You can go to bed," he said sharply, "and take your mother with you. She has been balking too much." "Oh, Francis, I fee/ so well." "You will be ill to-morrow if you don't go now," he said fondly. "I know rest is what you require, my own. Now go, darling." It seemed to Dora that her father wished to get them both out of the room and to bed, and she saw that the troubled look was still in his eyes. "There is nothing wrong, I hope?" she asked.</p>
        <p>. -- He started, and turned on her savagely. "There is nothing wrong," he repeated. "Good heavens! what should be wrong? But it is ten o'clock, and your mother should be in bed. I don't want her to have one of her bad days to-morrow. Mrs. Bedowen is back, and the servants are going to bed. I wish you to go also. That is plain enough, is it not?" "Will you come, Francis?" asked Mrs. Tre,- raewan, who had gathered together all her pos- sessions, including the pug. "sot yet, dear. I am going to my study to do some work. I. will "—he here broke off with a swift glance at his daughter, and then some- what sharply asked her why she did not give her arm to her mother. But Mrs. Tremewan proved refractory. She insisted that Francis should take her up the stairs. This he accordingly did, for he would as soon have thought of cutting off his head as of disputing an order given by his idol. At the bedroom door he kissed her hand again and went downstairs. Shortly afterwards Mrs. Bedowen and the servants came up to their rooms. Dora caught a glimpse of the house- keeper's face, and noticed that she looked worn, which was unusual with her, and set Dora won- dering. She connected it with her father's trouble, for in spite of his denial she was certain he was worried over some private affair. But it was useless to think further of the matter at this hour, so . . . she said good-night to her mother, and went to her own room. As she closed the door the hall clock struck ten. Her bedroom was on the side of the house fronting the common, and had a corner window which looked out on to the high road. The shutterr were closed as usual, but in spite of the chilly night Dora felt that the room was close. It *as raining when she looked out, and the night was impenetrably dark. She opened only one window looking on to the road, and by craning her neck she could see the light streaming from her father's study. Evidently he was working there as he had said, but why he should have the shutters open at so late an hour on so wild a night Dora could not understand. She bee-an to feel vaguely nervous, but knowing that, her father always had a good reason for what he did she dismissed her fears, and went to bed. In her present frame of mind she would have preferred the shutters to be open, but Tremewan WU'S</p>
        <p>arways very strict on that point, so hetore step- ping into bed Dora closed them. Then she rolled herself up with a sigh and tried to sleep. ;Me poor girl was very unhappy. She was of an affectionate nature, yet had no one to love save Will, and he was far away. Her father and mother were wrapped up in one another, and from tt.A e5‘.....-remiiitzu Ora er.-ninst Dora saw that it was useless to /lope _tor any affection from them. The two were absolutely selfish, and she had to stand aside and watch their happiness. The reason given by Mrs. Tremewan, to account for her husband's dislike of his daughter seemed to Dora to be hopelessly inadequate and unfair. Why should she be blamed because her mother had nearly died in bringing her into the world? She could find no reply to this, question, nor in- deed to the other upon which she pondered. And that other was why should she be forbidden to marry Will on account of a feud between their respective parents. She had no fancy for enact- ing her part in a Montague and Ca.pulet play in Devonshire. If Will's inheritance could have been preserved she would certainly have married him and defied her father. But the result would have been that Mr. Hamiaford, senior, would have cut off his son with a shilling. And for poverty Dora well knew her lover was not fitted. There was nothing for it but .to give him up. But how could she when she loved him so pas- sionately? Thus thinking Dora tossed and turned unable to sleep. Suddenly she sat up in bed. It seemed to her that she heard someone in the passage. After a pause she got out of bed and tried to open the door. She could not. It was fast closed. In a tremor of nervous excitement she lighted her candle and examined the door. The key was in the lock inside, but she had not turned it. Yet the door would not open. It was very strange. She went to the window and threw wide the shutters. From the study on the ground floor Streamed a light, from which she concluded that her father was still up. As she looked the figure 'of a man came from amongst the gorse bushes, and, crossing the stream of light, entered by the window. This, then, was the reason her father had sat up and had sent them all to bed. He expected a visitor. Who it was Dora, of course, could not guess. Besides, it was not her busi- ness. She closed the shutters again and returned to bed. _</p>
        <p>With regard to the door, she made no further attempt to open it. It just occurred to her that anxious no one should disturb his interview with his visitor her father had fastened the door in some way on the outside. Before dawn he would doubtless release her. It was useless to ask if he had fastened the door, as he wag the last person to tell her. Besides Tremewan was queer in many ways, and did many things which his daughter thought decidedly eccentric. Often she wondered if he were in his right mind. After a time she grew weary of trying to fathom the meaning of it all, and fell into an uneasy slumber. She was awakened after she had been asleep a few moments—according to her own com- putation. But apparently she had slept for hours, since it was now broad daylight. The shutters of the windows were still closed, but she could see the clear daylight between the chinks. But it was not at this she looked, but at Mrs. Bedowen, who, with a face as white as a sheet, Was standing in the doorway. "What is the matter?" cried Dora, sitting up oticidenly. "Your father," stuttered Mrs. Bedowen, hardly lehle to frame the words, "he is dead. I think he has been—been murdered."</p>
        <p>[Te be Continued.]</p>
      </div>
