      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>BEST&amp; GOES FARTHEST</head>
        <p>BEST&amp; GOES FARTHEST</p>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY CHAMPIONSHIP, Shrubl? First Man and Birchfield</p>
        <p>First Club.</p>
        <p>That A. Shrnbh, of the South London Harriers, is one of the finest cross-country runners who ever lived received ample proof on Saturday atHa_ydockPark,when for the third time in succession • the Horsham athlete 'VIVA the National individual championship from a field of 116 runners, He had, with the exception of Cl. Smith, the Midland championship, C. S. Silsby, and Cl. Pearce, the pick of England's long distance athletes to face, but he ran away from them all, and won as he liked in 59min 56 3-ssec., a wonderful performance over ten miles of heavy country, a fifth of which was over a heavy ploughed land. His nearest attendant was Sid J. Robinson of the Salford Harriers, the Northampton runner, who has twice won this paraeular race and run second once, while next in order came W. A. Mercer and J. Hosker both members of the Farriworth (Widnes Harriers). The surprise of the race, however, was the forward running of T. Edwards, of the Manchester Harriers, a -youth of twenty years, who was making his first essay In an event of the kind. Less than two miles fromhome he was second to Shrubb, lying well ahead of the others, and but for a partial breakdown would undoubtedly have oecupied that position atthe close. The club 'honours were a great disappointment to the South, for of the three clubs who made the journey from London, the holders, Highgate, who won the Southern championship so readily, had to be content with fourth position. South London were ninth, their next two men after Shrubb being F. A. Knott fifty- eighth, andthe famous sculler H. T. Blackstaffe, sixty- eighth, while Essex'Beagles, the most powerful club in - the South for many years, did not even finish a team. Birchfield thoroughly deserved their victory, while s Salford Harriers and Farnworth made a splendid race A for senond place. Thoplacings were : '...:Position</p>
