      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>FRAUDULENT TEA TRAVELLER. Latchford Ladies Imposed Upon. A Singular Case.</head>
        <p>A FRAUDULENT TEA TRAVELLER. Latchford Ladies Imposed Upon. A Singular Case.</p>
        <p>The magistrates sitting at Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday heard a case in which some revelations were made concerning the action of a man named Albert Prince alias Alfred Taylor, who 'vas arrested at Lymm on a charge of obtaining money by false pretenoles. Mr. J. C. Parr was thechairrnan of the Bench and there were also present on the Bench, Mr. William Long, Mr. W. Niald and Mr. J. Knowles. The prisoner was charged with obtaining 4s. in money by false pretences from Mary Jane Roberts, wife of Mr. Henry Roberts, J.P., of Rock Villas, Latchford, on February 23rd, and also with obtaining Bs. by false pretences from Miss Jane Fair- dough, of Latchford, on the same date. The prisoner pleaded guilty to the charges. He stated that he had the goods ready packed for delivery 7 but he was prevented from delivering them owing to his being arrested. In answer to the Clerk, the prisoner said he preferred to be tried at that Court and not at the Sessions. Mrs. Roberts was then called as a witness. Shestated that on the 23rd February the prisoner came to her house soliciting orders for tea. He said he had been recently employed by Lipton's, who were reducing their staff,and he was now working for the Ceylon and China, Tea Company, of 25, Mincing-lane, London. He pro- duced a price list and said that if he obtained 50 orders during his week's trial for the company, he would have a good appointment offered him. She gave him an order for two pounds of tea, and paid 4s. The tea wys to have been delivered on the fallowing Thursday, February 26th. He told her that his name was Taylor, and that he resided in Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath. The Clark: Did the tea arrive on Thursday, the 26th?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>FERGUS HUME.</head>
        <p>FERGUS HUME.</p>
        <p>Author of " The Mystery of a Hansom Cab," "The Pagan's Cup," "The Crime of the Crystal," " Woman—the Sphinx."-etc.. etc., etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER, V.</p>
        <p>re TERRIBLE NIGHT,</p>
        <p>'About the same time as Max and Mrs. Bedow'eft were discussing their evil scheme Mrs. Tremewan and Dora were spending the evening together in quite a friendly fashion. As a rule Tremewan took up so much of his wife's time that she had comparatively few moments for Dora. But on this occasion he had gone out to play whist with Colonel Gillard, and would not be back till after nine o'clock. Mrs. Tremewan found the evening was too long to pass alone, so she *told Dora to come and read to her, which the girl was willing enough to do. She was delighted to have her mother to herself for a time. For a long time Dora read chapter after chapter of a new novel which was so fashionable as to be dull. She herself did not care for that class of literature, but Mrs. Tremewan liked to hear about the doings of Dukes and Duchesses. It. was strare that she should have such a taste as she was a well- born woman and should have been above such a snobbish feeling. However, her taste in fiction was the fashionable novel, and Dora, stifling her yawns, read the rubbish as well as she was able. It was nine o'clock when she laid down the book.</p>
        <p>"I don't think much of that story," she said, yawning. "Oh, Dora, I think it is beautiful," cried Mrs. Tremewan with a flushed cheek; "the courage of the hero, and that poor Countess. No wonder he was willing to die for her." "Is a man ever willing to the for a woman?" asked Dora the sceptic. _ .</p>
        <p>'Certainly," said Mrs. Tremewan, with dignity, "and I can speak as one having some authority. Dora,' she added impressively, "in my youth I had many lovers, as I have often told you. There were three of them whom I am sure would have laid down their lives for me."</p>
        <p>Dora laughed, but she quite believed her mother's assertion. In the softly-shaded candle- light Mrs. Tremewan looked wonderfully young and charming—quite the sort of ethereal delicate beauty which would drive a man crazy. "I don't wonder, mother," she said with conviction; "you must have been the belle of Devonshire, let alone Dartbridge." Mrs. Tremewan blushed, well accustomed to compliments though she was. "I was not only a. belle here, Dora,' she said, "but in London, too, I made a success. Dear me, what a happy time I had. And those three were so deeply in love with me. Often and often I thought they would kill one another. It was rather a difficult posi- tion, Dora, for if I looked at one the other would be sure to complain. In my presence they scowled at one another like so many Corsairs.' "How dreadful," said Miss Tremewan, "and how nice. Men would never rave over me like that. Who were the three, mother?" "Your father was one dear. I married him after I returned from London. He vowed he would kill himself if I did not. Then there was Mr. Hannaford—he wore mourning for a year because he said I had jilted him." "Oh," cried Dora, suddenly enlightened. "Is that the reason my father and Mr. Hannaford can't get on well together?" Mrs. Tremewan nodded. "That is the reason. George Hannaford afterwards married, as you know; but although his wife is dead he has never forgiven me or your father. For over twenty years, Dora, lie has not spoken to us. So you may guess how angry he was to hear Will was in love with you. He'll never consent— never." _ _</p>
        <p>"No, I don't think he will," replied Dora, ruefully. "When a man is so vindictive as to bear malice for all those years he must have too Wong a will to be broken. lam afraid Will and I will never marry." "Don't be too sure, dear," said Mrs. Tremewan, patting her hand. "I have often thought that if I called to see George that is Mr. Haonaford —he might give way. "But what woukl father say?" "Oh, he would never forgive me, never. I fear Dora it wouldn't do. I wish I could bring you and Will together, bust you see I can do so little. Yet I should like to see George again," she sighed. "He was always very nice. I never see him, except in church sometimes. He is quite grey now. He doesn't keep his looks as I</p>
        <p>"You have the secret of perennial youth, dear mother," said Dora, kissing the flushed cheek. "You like Will, do you not?" "Yes, Dora," whispered the faded belle, w:th a glance at the door; but I never dared to say so before your father. You have no idea how jealous he is." "Hare I not," said the girl a trifle bitterly. "Why, mother, he is even jealous of me,—your own child."</p>
        <p>"I know he is. You see, Dora, when you were born I nearly died, and your father was in such a state that he could scarcely bear the sight of you afterwards. But he means to be kind dear." "Ohs So that is the reason he does not love me?"</p>
        <p>"I think it is. Francis is such a strange man. He has only love enough for one person, and he has given it all to me. He is so fierce sometimes that he frightens me. But I know that he loves me with all his heart. He has been such a good husband, dear. Never an unkind word or look. Never a day of neglect—not even a minute. Olio I have much to be thankful for." And what about me, mother?"</p>
        <p>" Oh, your father is fond enough of you in his own Way. I'm sure I don't know', why he shouldn't be. Of course, I did nearly die and all that. But I got better, and you are all right, to I don't think he should be unkind. And I'm sure he doesn't mean it," said poor Mrs. Tremewan in- coherently. "Don't look so sad, Dora. It's not my fault."</p>
        <p>-No, it certainly was not the fault of this frivolous little woman, who was kind enough in her own silly way. But Dora wondered how her father could be so unjust as to dislike his own child on such a weak plea. And yet, perhaps, after all, she put the case too harshly. He did not actitally dislike her, but he was indifferent to her. She was no more to him than a. dog or a cat. All his life was centred in this weak little beauty who had captured his heart in his youth. That he still loved her with the fierce devotion of twenty-one was proved by his still being jealous of his ancient rival George Hannaford. "I do wish father would let me marry Will," said Dora, after a pause; "he doesn't want me in the house so long as he has you mother. Why _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>should he deny me- my share Of happiness?" "He would be very glad to see you married if it was anyone else, said Mrs. Tremewan sooth- ingly; "but I told you he would never let you marry George Hannaford's son. If some other -" • "There is only one man in the world for me," said Dora, shaking her 'head, "and that is Will. I shall never marry anyone else." "Then I'm afraid you'll end up in being an old maid," said Mrs. Tremewan a trifle peeviAily, "and that will be horrid. Your father will iievEr consent to your marrying Will; never. If he knew that I had been even talking about the boy he would be seriously angry."</p>
        <p>'Then let us say no more about him," replied Dora, "but I don't need you to help me mother. If I marry Will Idoitby my own efforts. If Mr. Hannaford were only willing I should many without my father's consent."</p>
        <p>"Neither of them will consent," cried Mr. Tremewan, "they are both so olYitinatc. Don't let us talk any more Dora. Your father would be so angry." "I don't think he would he angry with you," replied Dora with a curl of her lip; "it is on me he would visit his _wrath. You are all riat mother. But let us change the subject. Who was the third lover?"</p>
        <p>Mrs. Tremewan, who had taken up her knitting again, let a smile play over her lips as it the question awa.kentd agreeable recollections. "He wa.s Walter Pillage," she said, softly, "and the handsomest of them all." "I thought the Pillage family were ert.,iot</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>CORONER AND COLLIERY MANAGER. Strong Comments by Mr. Brighouse. Fatality at Bold.</head>
        <p>CORONER AND COLLIERY MANAGER. Strong Comments by Mr. Brighouse. Fatality at Bold.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at the - Boundary Vaults, St. Helens Junction, on the body of Robert Williams, 24 years of age, a collier,of 20, Taylor- street,. who was killed at the Bold Colliery of the Collins Green Colliery Company on Wednesday night. Mr. Matthews, Government inspector of mines, was present, and Mr. E. Peace represented Mr. Thompson, certificated manager of the mine. Mr. W. Southern, secretary of the company, was also in attendance. The evidence showed that deceased and other men were working under the usual conditions on Wednesday night when a portion of the roof gave way add Williams was killed.—The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
        <p>lathe course of the proceedings, the Coroner stated that he had received a letter from Police-superin- tendent Baxendale to the effect that at 9.45 on Wednes day night he received a telephonic message from Mr Southern, secretary of the company, referring to the accident, and adding that the coroner had said the police could get ajury at any time in Bold within two hours to view a body, and asking that one might be got together for that purpose. The body, the message continued, was to remain at the colliery for the jury to view it until six o'clock next morning, when it would be removed home. Superintendent Baxendale added, in his letter, that it was impossible to get a jury together at that hour of the night. _</p>
        <p>--13-Olice-consta-ble Howells, at the coroner's request,said that he was told of the accident at ten o'clock at night. He was then in bed, but got up and walked three and a half miles to the colliery, and as he went along he warned the jury. All but one of the jurors were in bed at the time, and he had to knock them up. (Laughter). In consequence he was on duty for 26hours at a stretch. In reply to the coroner, Mr. Southern said he had acted in the matter on the direct instructions of Mr. Fairclough, the managing director of the company. The Coroner commented very strongly upon these statements. He said the Collins Green Company was the only company he did not seem to be able to get on with. When they last had trouble upon that matter it was agreed that the body should be kept at the colliery long enough to give the jury a reasonable opportunity of viewing it; but that had not been,done in that case when the circumstances were considered. It was a mis- demeanour for anyone to obstruct the coroner and his jury in their viewing of bodies. In that case the jury met at ten o'clock on the morning following the acci- dent, and found that it had been removed to the man's home at Sutton, and they had to be conveyed there in a waggonette. He would allow the matter to pass that time, but when another fatality occurred, if his wishes were not observed and the jury were not allowed a reasonable time to view the body, he would have Mr. Fairclough proceeded against. Everyone had been put to great inconvenience.</p>
        <p>Fitis r Pale People</p>
        <p>Cje-</p>
      </div>
