      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TIM ITNION CASTLE " NORMAN " WHICH HAS BROUGHT MR. CHAMBERLAIN FROM SOUTH AFRICA</head>
        <p>TIM ITNION CASTLE " NORMAN " WHICH HAS BROUGHT MR. CHAMBERLAIN FROM SOUTH AFRICA</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S RETURN.</p>
        <p>The arrival of the Norman with Mr. Chamberlain 1 board is being looked forward to with the Utmost interest throughout the country : and South- tt!aPton is preparing a great welcome for him. "51tmingham, as is to be expected, is also arranging to do him honour; and the City of London, which cannot confer the freedom of the City on him as it has already been given, is, through the Lord Mayor and Corporation, giving an address and a reception later in the month. There is no more controversial subject of discussion than Mr. Chamberlain, his Inethods and his views: but. there is no doubt that he has added an enormous number of friends and made no enemies by his statesmanlike trip through the neW British Colonies. Nothing in the whole parliamentary session is being so much looked forward to on every side as the Colonial Secretary's reappearance in the Home, and his speech on hi 7 tour and its results.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LATE MEMBER FOR CHERTSEY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MEMBER FOR CHERTSEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Currie Leigh-Bennett. the member for the Chertsey divisioni of 'Surrey, died on Saturday night last at the Windlesham residence of Mr. J. A. Tyler, the Conservative agent for the constituency. Mr. Leigh-Bennett. who succumbed after an operation for appen- dicitis, was born in 1852. He was the eldest son of the late Rev. Henry L e i*g h-Bennett, and was born at Thorpe . Place, Chertsey. He was educated at Win- chester and New College, Oxford, and was called to the bar in 1878. He was a mem- ber of the Surrey County Council, Chairman of the .Chertsey Rural District Council •and the Thorpe Parish Council• Was a Fellow of THE LATE MR. H. C. LEIG the Royal Society BENNETT, M.P. of Literature and a director of the London and South-Western Railway. Mr. Leigh-Bennett had tePresented the Cherisey Division in the Conser- Vative interest since 18D7, when at a bye-election he -defeated the Liberal candidate by 868 votes. He bad not followed his calling as a barrister fora Iluniber of years, interesting him,elf chiefly in Ocal affairs. He was. however, an ardent sports- Zan, especially fond of cricket. He was a devotee a,,Jhe old Masters, and was something of an su-mrian He married. in 178. Florence • utgale, the third daug-14er of Tho!flas MiLler wackay. He was a very popular mln with all hcgn he came in contact, and his death is very Widely regretted.</p>
