      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NEW NAVAL BASE</head>
        <p>THE NEW NAVAL BASE</p>
        <p>The announcement that the Government 11(12 determined to establi:iii a naval port orei base in. St. Margaret's Hope, on the north eide of the Firth of Forth has been favourably. received all round.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>spot which</head>
        <p>The spot which</p>
        <p>,s'en i a natural har-</p>
        <p>hour Of refuge, Nl hich has exisled for centuries There is no other place of sh Humber and Aberdeen. There is capilat anchor- age, and as many as 200 ye eis.hLve occupied it t 4 'one time. In the Forth there is pleut,. of deep Water all the way up. If the Government are ta. king Inverkeithing as well, it will be a di.Etinctly desirable acquisition. This is a mile Wide, two miles long, and not more than l:;0 to</p>
        <p>Ather lx.tween tne</p>
        <p>THE FIRTH OF FORTH</p>
        <p>SHOWING ST. MARGARET'</p>
        <p>HOPE, THE NEW BASE,</p>
        <p>200 yards wide at the entrance. There is a clay bottom, 20 feet in depth, and altogether it will make an excellent harbour for repairing purposes. Within the last five years batteries have been erected, and there is also an electrical station for firing torpedoes. It is understood that the Govern- tnent has made sufficient purchases along the fore- shore of St. Margaret's Hope to obviate the neces- sity of paying an enhanced price for the land. The site was practically chosen more than twelve months ago, and the plans of the Admiralty are now so advanced that work will be commeiued in the spring. The first task to be undertaken' will be the construction of a dry dock and a repairing yard.</p>
