      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WONDERFUL LADY VIOLINIST.</head>
        <p>THE WONDERFUL LADY VIOLINIST.</p>
        <p>Miss Marie Hall plays the violin. How she plays it may be gathered from the fact that for her second appearance in public, which was at the St. James's Hall, London, last --week, the place was packed with an eager crowd of music- lovers long before the concert began, while the ,corridors were thronged with late comers offering reckless prices for seats in any part of the hall, or even for a foot DT so of standing room. Miss Hall is not only a " virtuoi:o " o f the very highest rank, but a, musician of ex- quisite tempera- ment. At her first concert she proved that sh.. had nothinf., to learn in the w: of technique, till the Kreutzet Sonata, whiett she attenlpted at her second, witnt something tuoi.e than technique. SPHO HAS PROBABLY 1W) Tin, It would not I,t- -awn SUCCESS EVER AC- surnyss CORD ED A MUSICIAN. true to say</p>
        <p>MISS MARIE HALL,</p>
        <p>11111 yet the Beethoven player that she may one day become. She is only eighteen years old, nuct she is liked all the more because she plays like a girl of eighteen and not like a woman of forty. Her Playing of the Kreutzer Sonata is charmingly fresh and delicate: it has not the profundity of emotion, nor the virile exaltation which years and experi- ence will bestow but it has a definite beauty of its own, and one which proves Miss Hall to have a curiously alert and sympathetic musical instinct.</p>
