      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>COMING MOTOR RACE</head>
        <p>THE COMING MOTOR RACE</p>
        <p>General satisfaction is being expressed at the practical certainty that the Bill to permit the race for the Gordon Bennett Trophy in Ireland will go through the Houses of Parliament unopposed. The motoring industry, which a few years back was so despised, is now becoming a powerful trade factor and has in consequence forced recognition on every side, and now motoring is the chief hobby of many people. This particular race, which was formed by-Mr. Gordon Bennett, of the "New York Herald,' is a contest between any countries that hrve an Automobile Club of their own, and are recognised</p>
        <p>I ihill-M111411M1111441(Ltx111111tP THE GORDON BENNETT TROPHY.</p>
        <p>by the other club. The course must be not less than 344 miles long and not more than 416: and the winner of the year before has the right to hold the race the following year in his own country. All cars must be entirely built and finished in theft own native country. Last year Mr. S. F. Edge won the cup on a Napier caroo this year the race has to take place in Britain. The course chosen is in the Irish midlands, beginning and ending in the town of Naas. The course is 116 A miles, and will therefore have to be traversed three times by the contesting cars. The Americans have entered three Wintons, France two Panhards and a Mars, Germany three Mercedes and England three Napiers, and the drivers will include Messrs. Winton, de Knyff, Fournier, Jarrott, Edge, and other well known names. It is to he followed by a general tour of cars round Ireland : and it is thought that it will be a very good thing financially to many of the poorer districts. The miler greai automobile event of the year is the Paris to Madrid race in which 250 cars .at least will start. The roads in Ireland are reported excellent on the whole, but those in Spain are said to be rough and dangerous.</p>
