      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DUBLIN UNIVERSITY'S NEW MEMBER.</head>
        <p>DUBLIN UNIVERSITY'S NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>The new member for Dublin University, Mt James H. M. Campbell, 'C.C.., is S3licitor-0 ener.l: for Ireland. He is"the youngest son of the lat, Mr. William M. Campbell, a late Chief Superin- tendent of the Dublin Metropolitan Polke, and is nearly fifty-two year of age. He was edm cated at Dr. Stacpoole's Kingstown School and at Trinity College, Dublin, which he entered in 1870, and</p>
        <p>where he had a</p>
        <p>very distin- guished univer- sity career, being</p>
        <p>a -sizar, scholar</p>
        <p>, and double gold medallist? of</p>
        <p>Trinity College,</p>
        <p>Vice-dhancellOi's'</p>
        <p>gold medallist, and gold medal- list for oratory</p>
        <p>and history. He</p>
        <p>was called to the</p>
        <p>Irish Bar in 1878</p>
        <p>joining the</p>
        <p>North-Eat Cir-</p>
        <p>cuit, was ap- pointed a Queen's MB. JAMES, IL M. CAMPBELL, Counsel in 1892, and subsequently THE NEW MRAITI UR FOR DUBLIN elected a Bencher 17:NIVER;SITY.</p>
        <p>of the King's Inns. In 1899 he was called to nu English Bar at Gray's Inn, of which he was las. year elected a Bencher. Be has held nnn official positions in Ireland, hits been Crowt Prosecutor for the counties of Antrim am.' Armagh and at Green . Street, Dublin, and sine( 1901 Solicitor-General for Ireland. Ho.represenied St. Stephen's-green Division of Dublin from the elevation of Mr. William Kenny to the Irish Bencl: in 1898 until the General Election of 1900.</p>
