      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TWO CRICKETERS INDISPOSED,</head>
        <p>TWO CRICKETERS INDISPOSED,</p>
        <p>As the spring begins to make itself felt, the vast majority of people in England commence to turn their thoughts to the approaching cricket season, which is as popular as it is short, compared with the innings of football. This year there will be nc Australian team or series of test matches to Work the public up to the top point of International en- thusiasm; but there is always the County Cham- pionship with us, which excites unbounded local interest from associations of patriotism. This is always most keenly contested; and though for many years now Yorkshire have had the monopoly of the champion honours, the other counties still continue to live in hope that they will be able to wrest them from them in another season. And they</p>
        <p>ARTHUR SHREWSBURY.</p>
        <p>TYLDESLF.Y.</p>
        <p>TWO CRICKETERS AT PLLESENT ON THE SICK LIST,</p>
        <p>have no cause to complain of local backing, -how ever scant their successes, an item of news which is interesting all cricket enthuia.its, is that of the illness of two very prominent county champions of high standing. The first is Shrewsbury, the well- known Notts professional, whose batting was at one time the finest in he country, and is even now not far off the top. He has been very unwell, and it was at one time feared that he would not be able to play cricket again; but now the news is more hopeful, and it is probable that he will be able to turn out again for his old county. The other is Tyldesley, the prominent Lancashire pro., who is not only a fine bat, but is the finest field in the country ; a point which enhances his value con- siderably. He has had some trouble with a varicose vein in his left leg, but it has been suc- cessfully removed, and it is thought that he will be quite all right again before the commencement of the cricket season. The great point of discussion at the moment is the proposal to enlarge the wicket, to place batsmen and bowlers on fairer terms.</p>
