      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. JOHN COX. Fatal Fall in a Bathroom.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOHN COX. Fatal Fall in a Bathroom.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. John Cox, 17, Palmyra Square, Warrington who passed away at his residence at 12.15 a.m. on Sunday. The deceased gentleman, who was 56 years of age, was very well known in the town, having been a traveller for Messrs M. Bowden and Sons, grocers Bre, Sankey-street for 27 years. In politics he was a Conservative being a member of the House Committee of the Conservative club. He should have been present at the distribution of the billiard handicap prizes at the Club on Thursday week but sent a letter regretting his inability to be pre- sent owing indisposition. Mr Cox was a sidesman at Holy Trinity Church and was a member of the " Prince of Powys" Lodge of the Manchester Unity, Independent Order of Oddfellows. He leaves a widow two daughters and a son with whom much sympathy is expressed. in their bereavement. The circumstaNces attending Mr. Cox's death were somewhat peculiar, and the facts being reported to the Coroner it was deemed necessary to hold an inguest. The deceased gentleman met with an accident in his bathroom on February 25th, and blood poisoning supervened. Dr. Joseph was his medical attendant and at the inquest stated that blood poisoning was the cause of death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>INQUEST.</head>
        <p>THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Tuesday afternoon by Mr. F. A. Jones, Deputy Coroner. Miss Jane Cox stated that at 7.40 a.m. on February 25th her father got up and went to the bathroom to have his usual bath. Shortly afterwards she heard a thud, and on going into the room found him lying on the floor, bleeding from a wound on the right hand, and inn dazed condition. She raised him and bound up the hand, and in a short time he was able to go to Dr. Joseph who dressed the wound. He did not appear much worse until the following Wednesday when he complained of a pain in his back. He went to bed and Dr. Joseph was sent for and attended him till death took place on Sunday morning last. During the morn- ing of the accident witness asked deceased how it happened, and he said he was standing on a box near the window when he slipped and fell against a chest of wooden drawers.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph said the deceased came to see him on the 25th ult., and witness found he had a lacerated wound about an inch long between the third and fourth finger of the right hand. He said he had fallen on his back from a box in the bathroom and had caught his hand against some drawers Witness asked him if he had hurt his back and h replied "Not much." The following Wednesday wit ness was sent for to dress the wound which had healed very well except in one place. Some matter was oozing from it. Deceased complained of pain in his right hip and on examining it witness found it was discoloured. Later on the discolonration extended to the knee and calf of the leg. Witness had made a post-mortem examination of the body assisted by Drs. Fox and Smith. Death was due to blood poisoning, caused by rupture of a vein in the hip. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and the Foreman said that most of the jury knew the deceased, and wished to express their sympathy with the widow and family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>FUNERAL</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon the remains of the deceased gentleman were interred in the Warrington Cemetery in the presence of manyrelatives and friends. The officiat- ing clergymen were the Revs. E. C. E. Carleton and A. Inglis. The chief mourners were Mrs. Cox (the widow), Mr. John Cox (son), Misses L. and J. Cox (daughters), Mrs. Reece (sister), Mr. Albert Reece (nephew), Mr. and Mrs. Simons (brother-in-law and sister), Mr. Robert Galliers (nephew) and Miss N. Garners (niece). Mr. A. Bowden attended on behalf of Messrs. M. Bowden and Sons, and the Conservative Association and clubs were represented by Messrs. George Watkins (manager of the senior club), Herbert Hayes (manager of the junior club), George Jolley, T. H. Ashcroft, G. Pilling, D. Rawlins, and 'W. Holbrook (Conservative agent). Amongst those present were Messrs. John Halton, and James Harding (representing Holy Trinity Church), T. B. Potter, P.C. Coates, Whittle, Rowe and Waldron. Floral tributes were sent by the following :—The widow and daughters, Mr. and Mrs. J. Cox, jun.,Mr. and Mrs. Simons, Mrs Reece and family, Mrs. Galliers and family, the House Committee of the Conservative Club, the second class room at the Conservative Club, Mr. and Mrs. Watkins, "His fellow workmen," Mr. and Mrs. John J. Dunn, Mr. and Mrs. Birtles, Mr. C. B. Sutton, Miss M. Price, Dr. and Mrs. Kisby, Mrs. Pierpoint and family, the Misses Mather, the Misses and Master Williams.</p>
        <p>The Licence of the Mulberry Tree Inn. On Tuesday the adjourned licensing sessions for the Daresbury Division were held before Mr. J. C. Parr (chairman) Mr. W. Long, Mr. H. Nield and Mr. Joseph Knowles: Mr. Draper, of Messrs Robert Davies and Company, solicitors appeared in support of an application made by Mrs.Newall for the renewal of the licence of the Mulberry Tree lim,Stockton Heath. He stated that it was put back at the licensing sessions on account of the back door arrangements of the cottages not being satisfactory to the Chief Constable. Under the scheme of the Warrington Light Railway four out of the six cottages would come down so that it would to a great extent meet the objection. He I understood that the inquiries had been held before the Light Railway Commissioners and a notification had I been received from the Bbard of Trade that the order had been granted although the order itself had not actually been received. The plans and specifications had been got out and the estimates had been received for the work. Immediately the order was received the work would be proceeded with. In order to meet the views of the Bench, he (Mr. Draper) with Messrs. John White Maolver and Co., met Superintendent Eianion on the ground with the view of coming to some arrangement to do away with the objection of the Chief Constable until the scheme was carried through. Plans had been prepared showing what was proposed to be done, and he would submit them to the Bench. The walls had been raised two feet but the Chief Constable desired another foot to be added. A door giving access to the back from the cottages was to be closed, and a fresh doorway was to be made, so that the tenants could go through the passage at the side entrance.—The Chairman: That will meet the objection of the police ?—Superintend- ent Ennion : It does, sir.—The licence was re- newed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>FOR WEARY MILL WORKERS. A Lancashire Woman Cured. WONDERFUL EFFECT OF BILE BEANS. Indigestion, Biliousness, Weakness and Headache Banished.</head>
        <p>FOR WEARY MILL WORKERS. A Lancashire Woman Cured. WONDERFUL EFFECT OF BILE BEANS. Indigestion, Biliousness, Weakness and Headache Banished.</p>
        <p>If you are a worker in a mill or factory you will have at sometime or other experienced the same feeling of weariness and weakness which troubled Mrs. Sarah Clough, of 30. Goddard-street, Oldham. Or perhaps you are suffering at this very moment? If so it will pay you to learn from the story which Mrs. Clough details how Chas. Forde's Bile Beans completely dispel all symptoms of weakness, all pangs of indigestion, and the many depressed feelings begotten of sick headache. i This s what Mrs. Clough told an "Oldham Standard" reporter. 'Sometime ago I was overtaken by the scourge of indigestion. I lost my appetite and any little food which I took always came back. I soon got so weak that I could do no work. 1 had no energy,myhead was heavy and I felt drowsy and lazy. 1 breathed with difficulty and I had pain in the chest, and bilious head- aches. It gave me much trouble to get about, and often I lay awake for hours at night. At length I got so bad that I dared not go out, for the effort of getting along the street was too much fot me. I took various medioines which were recommended to me, yet nothing did me the least good until I tried Chas. Ford's Bile</p>
        <p>- - I purchased a supply of them and I soon began to feel much better in consequence of their use. The pain disappeared, T ate and slept well. I could breathe more freely, and I no longer had any sick headache. Bile Beans have now put me as right as can be. I have been in excellent health ever since—as well as ever I was in my life—and now I find my work a pleasure to me. I have given some of the Beans to a neighbour, and they have set her up. There is no doubt that Chas.,Forde's Bile Beans are, a wonderful medicine, and Jean conscientiously recommend them to all who suffer as I did.l Chas. Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness, are without doubt superior to all known medicines for headache, indigestion liver and kidney disorders,- backache, chest, F pains, sickness, insomnia, constipation, piles, palpita- tion, debility, dizziness, colds, chill, influenza, rheu- matism, anwmia, and all female ailments. All chemists stock them, or you may obtain post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., Red Cross-street, London 8.C., by sending price is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box (2s. 9d box contains three times 1/lid. Insist on seeing the. name Chas. Forde on every box.</p>
        <p>Beans. • ~.. ,-, ...., "•4,4 i 1 ( 10/Wer% ' IF ........ 74ty</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION OP.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION OP.</p>
        <p>THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN V. MARYPORT.</p>
        <p>In the third round of the Northern Union Cup Tie the above clubs met at Runcorn on Saturday. The ground was vei#y heavy, there was a strong wind blow- ing and the sun was shining brightly when the teams filed on the ground. Richardson turned out in the threequarter line but Price had not recovered from the knock to his knee. Walker played at half-back. The visitors won the toss and elected to play with the sun and breeze behind them. The game opened out with Runcorn making tracks for the visitors' line, Moran nearly getting in with a neat run. The visitors' next got away. Houghton being called on Jolley re- lieved but the home contingent could not get out of their own quarters. Several fine chances were missed by Maryport, a penalty eventually working the leather out of danger. A smart round of passing, Richardson making the run, was spoiled by Jones mulling. Another chance was spoiled by Walker, but Houghton by a mis- chievous kick and run relieved, and found touch at the top corner. From the scrum Jolley set his line in motion, and after the ball had been touched byall in the rear division, Jones grounded at the corner, Farmer failing to improve. After desultory play Smith got going, only to have his career spoiled by Richardson. A shot at ,goal did not improve the visitors' chances, Jones picking up and gain- ing considerable ground. From the scrum Maryport gained possession but Moran taking the pass raced half way up the field and beat Davidson, grounding at the corner. The leather was hardly set in motion than Walker missed a splendid chance through failing to take the pass. The failure was, however, redeemed by a splendid break away initiated by Jolley and Richardson which enabled Moran to score between the posts. Farmer again failed at goal. After this the visitors woke up and a desperate attack on the Runcorn line re- sulted in the ball being kicked dead. Scrambling play followed,Runcom repeatedly gaining possession but fail- ing to get over the line. Moran with a dashing run nearly did the trick, being held on the line. Runcorn however, were not to be denied for Richardsonhandling the ball with fine style made a splendid run, passing to Jones who scored.. Farmer failed at goal. Re- suming Maryport kicked dead twice Runcorn having to defend. The check was but temporary for Jolley gathered from the scrum and beating his man handed to Butterworth who rounded his opponents and placed the ball between the.posts, Farmer landing a goal. Half- time :—Runcorn one goal, five tries, Maryport nil. At the change of ends Runcorn rushed away the visitors defending. Danger was averted, play being contested at centre and Richardson placed his side on the offensive, Robley in trying to collar him colliding with the touch flag and having to leave the field. Run- corn still pressed Houghton and Richardson being within an ace of scoring. The visitors defence was, however, very keen. After unexciting play Runcorn mulled and let the visitors in. Young got away and, beating Houghton, grounded between the posts David- son failing at goal. Maryport still pressed but a splen- did punt by Houghton found the visitors defending the lines. Several combined efforts by the Linnets were well broken up, Jolley next having a futile drop at goal. Uninteresting play continued to the close which arrived with Runcorn winning a poor game by seven- teen points to three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HULL v. MILLOIII.</head>
        <p>HULL v. MILLOIII.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before rather a small gate. The only score in the first half was It try by Moxon. The play was again keen in the second half, and Millom being pena- lised Cook scored a goal. This was followed by a dropped goal by Frank. Final :—Hull, seven points; Millom, nil. ROCHDALE HORNETS v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>A strong wind blew from goal to goal, and the Hornets had the good fortune to have it in their favour during the first half. Despite this, however, the Sal- ford forwards quickly began to get the better of their lighter opponents, which allowed Harter to set his backs in motion, and Lomas early on ssored an unim- proved try. The Hornets then played up strongly, and for the remainder of the first half the game was fought out in the Salford quarter. Baxter made two or three capital attempts to cross the visitors' line, but he found the defence of Smith and Lomas too strong, and when half-time arrived Salford led by three points to nothing. With the wind behind them Salford at once attacked strongly, and all through the second half the Hornets were on the defensive. They were completely, overplayed at all points, and in the end were easily de- feated. Lomas scored two more tries and Norris and Bell one each, but all the goal kicks failed. Salford this entered the fourth round by five tries (15 points) to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>HUNSLET v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>HUNSLET v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>Mosley commenced the game and Leeds attacked in earnest, brit good work by Whiteley resulted in J. Whiteley scoring, A. Goldthorpe converting. In the second half Leeds attacked strongly, and Littlewood kicked a goal. Only three points divided the teams, and each side played a great game, and the end came with Hunslet leading by one goal and one try (5 points) to Leeds one goal (two points). After the match a pro- test was lodged by Leeds on account of the field of play being encroached upon by the crowd. BARROW v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>In the first half Barrow played a great game, and Mellor scored after brilliant passing, the same player improving. Batley were then penalised, and Mellor kicked another goal, the interval score being :—Barrow, seven points; Batley, nothing. In the second half Davies kicked a penalty goal for Batley, and the final read :—Barrow, two goals and one try (seven points) ; Batley, one goal (tvo points).* HALIFAX v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS.—A point- less draw was the result of this fixture at Halifax. The home team had whstever advantage accrued from the boisterous wind which prevailed( Time after time they were within inches of scoring, but the desired result had not been attained at the interval, when nothing had been scored. Agabst thelwindHalifax did better,Little once hittin., the posts in a futile shot at goal. In the replay on Tuesday, Halifax won by 8 points to ; points. - BRADFORD V. OLDHAM.—This game at Bradford was not of an exciting character, there being far too much forward work. At half-time there was no score, but early in the second half Ferguson made a goal from a penalty. Bradford rarely had a look in though Oldham could not score again, the result being :—Oldham, one goal (2 points) : Bradford nothing. KzIGHLEY v. YORK.—At Keighley before 3000 spectators. Caine partnered Myers, who registered a penalty goal in the first half. In the second half Cain, retired hurt, and Eeighley had to concede a penalty</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>air g 11.11%</head>
        <p>air g 11.11%</p>
        <p>Grateful—Comforting.</p>
        <p>, A</p>
        <p>13reakfast—Supper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>THE LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. At Fartown,before 5,000 spectators,the visitors had the assistance of the elements in the first half, but the home men played surprisingly well, and more than held their own. No points, however, were scored in the first half. After the change of ends Huddersfield pressed, and Holroyd placed a goal. There was no further score. Final :--Huddersfield, one goal; Broughton Rangers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LEIGH v. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, before 2,000 spectators, in windy weather. In the first half, with a strong wind behind them, Leigh attacked repeatedly, but experienced very hard luck. Molyneux dropped a goal for Leigh. In the second half, St. Helens attacked, Barnes scoring an unimproved try by a dribble, and Hall dropping a goal. Liversage gained a try through Leigh mulling. Later, Ganley scored a try for the home side. Final :—St. Helens, 1 goal, 2 tries (8 points) ; Leigh, 1 goal,l try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>OLDHAM v. BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>This postponed fixture took place at Oldham en Tuesday in fine weather and before 5,000 people. Brad- ford pressed, and Roberts scored, Mosby adding a goal. Tyson scored a try for Oldham in the second half. Oldham did most of the attacking, and Rees missed a fine chance from a pass by Perguson. Dunbavin nearly dropped a goal for Bradford, who won cleverly. Result :—Bradford, one goal and one try (five points): Oldham, one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SWINTON v. WIGAN._</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. WIGAN._</p>
        <p>At Swinton, be-1c:W.3,66 spectators. Both sides made changes in the composition of the fifteens. Swinton played in the opening half with the aid of a powerful breeze, and this they utilised to much advantage. Pres- ton opened the scoring with a penalty goal. Then Hampson and Wallwork scored tries, one of which Preston improved, the home lot leading on crossing over by 10 points to nil. Swinton also had much the best of matters in the second half, and after Hampson had just failed to score, Lewis made amends by registering a try, and Preston improved the effort. Wallwork next scored, and Lewis before the end notched another try, but Preston's goal kicks on these occasions were unsuccessful. Just before time a for- ward from each team was ordered off. Swinton retired winners of a runaway game by 21 points to nothing. Final :—Swinton, three goals five tries (21 points) ; Wigan, nil.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. WARRINGTON "A" v. OLDHAM "A."</p>
        <p>The encounter between these teams on the Wilders- pool enclosure on Saturday preyed exceptionally interesting and attracted nearly 3,000 spectators. Each club played a strong team, Fish, Burgess, Morrison, and Boardman finding places in the Warrington ranks, whilst the Oldham representatives included Huzzey, the Welsh International, S; Williams, Lawton, and Bonser, who figured prominently in the county team three seasons ago, and James and Civil, the Northampton three- quarters. In the first half Warrington had the advan- tage of the strong wind, and soon became agressive. Morrison leading the forwards to within easy reach of the Oldham line. A clever sdefence was exhibited by Wood, but another warm attack saw Watkins held up on the line. Lawton cleared effectively, and after Fish had failed at a penalty. the Oldham backs combined effec- tively and forced Warrington back, Fish and Knight ex- hibiting a sound defence. Burgess eventually gave his side a footing in the Oldham quarters again, and Fish twice in quick succession was held when he appeared dangerous. Wood got his side out of a tight corner, but premature work by Lawton got his side penalized, and Fish landed a magnificent goal from near the half- way line. A fine run by Fish near the interval was warmly applauded, but Wood's defence was sound, and at half-time Warrington led by two points to nil. Old- ham attacked strongly in the concluding stage, but the defence of Knight, Fish, Bailey, and Watkins was very. safe. Nothing was scored during this half, and War- rington won a keenly contested game by a penalty goal to nil.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH. LATCHFORD RANGZRS V. WOOLSTON. Played at Warrington on Saturday. Latchford were the first to attack, and M`Wherter scored after clever passing. Following this success the Rangers had au i the play, and clever combination resulted 'in Phillips getting in, which Brown improved upon. Another try soon followed,and Latchford attacked until the interval. Half-time: Latchford, 16 points; Woolston, nil. During the second half the Rangers again proved them- selves to bathe smarter organization, and finally won by 20 points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE FIRST DIVISION. •</p>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON PARK V. LATCHFORD BRITANNIA . This match was played at Oughtrington Park on Sat- urday, and resulted in an easy victory for the home ,team by five goals to nil. STOCKTON HEATH V. NEW C HURCH.—Played St Stockton Heath on Saturday. During the first half ,play was even, and nothing was scored. In the concluding stage, the home side pressed throughout, and won easily by three goals to nil. WARRINGTON TRINITY V. L ATCHFORD .—These teams met on the ground of the first named on Saturday. In the early stages of this match, Latchford attacked and Muskett scored clevert,y. Shearer equalized immediately after, but the visitors retaliated, and Griffiths succeeded in putting his side ahead just before the interval, at which period the score read Latchford,two goals; Trinity 1 goal. In the second half the game was exciting throughout and both sides, notching a couple of goals, the match ended in a victory for Latchford by four goals to three. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. THELWALL. At half-time there was no score. Thelwall made better use of the wind in the second half, and scoring a goal, deservedly won a hard game. WARRINGTON " Gt. ARM AN " V. ST. PETER'S. On the ground of the latter on Saturday. Final :- 1 Guardian," two goals; St. Peter's, two.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE LEAGUE. EARLESTOWN V. CHORLEY. At Earlestown. Owing to the late arrival of the visitors, the start was delayed a quarter of an hou'r. Earlestown won the toss, and Hodkinson started before 800 spectators, a strong wind blowing across the ground. The homesters quickly assumed the aggressive, and from a long return by 'Pennington, Leach headed just wide of the mark. For a time the homesters monopo- lised the game, and from a pass by Barlow, Williams was left with a fine opening, buthe dallied too long,and Burben bustled him off the leather before he could get in his shot. A burst by Smith on the visitors' right looked ominous for the homesters, but Leigh effected a clearance in splendid fashion. Earlestown were at the other end again in a trice, and Taylor was lucky to get rid of a shot from Barclay. Half-time :—Earlestown 3, Chorley nil. Final :—Earlestown 3, Chorley 1.</p>
        <p>CHUM V. NEWTON. At Newton-le-Willows. Newton were short of Ellis, Chirk having full strength. The strong wind interfered with accurate play. Lee scored for Newton after some good play. Chirk had hard lines, the ball striking the upright. The visitors' combination was very good, Newton being hampered by Hill,whose injury prevented him doing his duty. Morris scored with a good shot for the visitors. Rudd topped the bar with a splendid effort, and Morris saved from Wilson. The wind spoiled play. Kenny was playing well, and forced a corner, from which Hill scored. Interval :—Newton, 2; Chirk, 1. Final: Newton, 3; Chirk, 1.</p>
        <p>A Fireman's Shop on Fire. . It is not often that a fireman is summoned to an outbrsak of fire on hi h own premises, but this was the. case on Tuesday evening when Mr. William Powell, who is a member of the Fire Brigade and carries on. the business of a fried fish and chip potato dealer at shops in Sankey-street and Kendrick-street, wan ; another part of the town and heard the fire bell ring. He at once proceeded to the fire station, and was ' naturally surprised, not to say alarmed, to hear that the fire had broken out at his shop in Sankey-street. He and a number of firemen lost no time in turning out with the reel cart, but on arriving at the shop they found that the flames had been extinguished. It appears that some woodwork adjoining one of the stoves had ignited, but fortunately little damage was done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
