      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MARCH 13th, 1903.</p>
        <p>IN the House of Corn- THE TRAINING OF mons on Monday even- OFFICERS. ing Colonel LONG raised an interesting debate on the training of officers. His motion ws much broader than either his ovvn speech or the subse- quent statement of the Secretary for War. It spoke of the "professional training of the officers and men of our army and auxiliary forces." But the men somehow slipped out of consideration, and it was the training of officers that was almost solely the subject of discussion. We may have a most perfect organisation on paper, said Colonel LONG, but it may be found on some sudden emergency to be a paper organisation only. An unexpected call is made upon it, and the army is found to be quite unable to cope with a well-trained adversary. This, as we know' was the first lessen pressed home to us by the South African war. As has been said by most of those experts who have the experience of that great event behind them, our officers knew how to do as they were told, but they had no initiative of their own. They were at a loss when they found themselves with nothing to rely upon but their own responsibility. Their bravery was un- doubted. They were ready to die, but they were not prepared to lead. In fact, they were without practical knowledge of their own profession. This was the melancholy conclusion at which the Specie Committee on the Training of Officers arrived As Colonel LONG pointed out, distinguished; officers of high position are unanimous in saying that junior officers are lamentably wanting not only in military knowledge but in desire to acquire it. The army is to a large extent officered by fashionable young men who are so far from being anxious to acquire a full knowledge of their calling that they resent the presence of the " sap" amongst them, and " rag" the young officer who devotes his time to the earnest study of his duties and the science of warfare. Colonel LONG asked for the establishment of a living chain. of responsibility from the top to the bottom of the' Army. He believes that if the senior officers are keen about their businebs the junior officers will imbibe their spirit and exhibit a keenness similarly praiseworthy. The debate which followed was of a quiet, friendly character. It was of the kind usually described as dull, but it was useful, and it illustrated the desire, now general in Parliament, we believe, to see our Army ebtablished on a better and more trustworthy basis. Mr. BRODRICK had a scheme to disclose. Its main feature is a new educational advisory Board in connection with the examination of candidates before they enter the Army. It is proposed that there shall be associated with the Director-General of EduCaTion a Board' consisting of the FolrlieZis of the military colleges of Woolwich, Sandhurst, OW, the staff College, and the Ordnance College.; two representatives of the Universities; one elected by the Incorporated Association of Headtnasters, one by the Royal Society. Thip last appointment is made with the view of securing the requisite attention to scientific attainments. The necessary</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <p>drawn up by the body thus aPpointed. The whole examination for Sand- hurst for the Army and for the Militia should Mr. BRODRICK thinks, be single examination only. But one year at Sandhurst is no longer to suffice. The rule must be, as at Woolwich, a two years' course, and if a candidate does not apply himself to the acquisition of the requisite knowledge during his two years, he will be asked to resign. There will be no mercy for the idler or the proved dullard: Up to this point the statement of the Secretary for War met with the approval' of Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN. The Lelder of the Opposition thought it would give satisfac- tion t 9 the House, as in fact it did, Colonel LONGS amendment being ultimately withdrawn without a division. Sir HENRY, however, pointed out that While he considers the plan of the Government on the whole judicious, it is based on the assumption that the Army mast. be closed, almost strictly closed so for as the higher branches are concerned, except to the leisured classes. We quite agree With the Liberal leader in thinking that it is " a Monstrous mistake " to practically close a military career to all but the sons of wealthy or patrician families. If this had been done in other countries, a(sue of the greatest soldiers of recent times would never have been heard of. Napoleon's ablest gstierals would have remained in the ranks. There Might even have been no Napoleon to make such a %in history. Military genius is as likely to from one rank of life as from another, the ePPortunities of gaining experience and the "mule to advancement being equal; but in this country we have agreed to believe that only a cc gentleman can lead the common soldier, and that le really the conviction which underlies, the state- Meld made by Mr. BRODRICK.</p>
        <p>Woolwich election result is an'ither nail in the Vfin of the Government, and that the nail should Qe driven by erstwhile friends and supporters doe 3 Uot lessen the bitterness of the fact. We can only ilzsgine what the dismay of Mr. Balfour and his adherents must have been when the dramatic erticulars of Mr. Crooks triumph came to hand. els figures are menacing in their uncompromising. "Luntnees. No bye-election in recent years has conveyed so unmistakable a warning to the Govern- ,l!l_eut of the day. The last contested election at W ' oolwich resulted in a majority of 2,805 against :t Labour candidate, while Wednesday's poll gave a Labour majority of 3,229—a turnover of no less than 6,034 votes. The metamorphosis can be explained but it cannot be explained away, and the most optimistic supporter of the Government must feel some dismay at the obvious turn Public opinion has taken. For Mr. Brodrick We think the situa' ion is exceptionally serious. The defection of Woolwich, the main depot for the army munitions must aeem like a "viper in the bosom " and the very place where he would expect his extravagant and I,auciful scheme of army reform to be viewed in Ihe rosiest light comes the harshest condemnation. e electors of Woolwich have, evidently torn the Veil from their eyes in good time and given their rurdy champion,Mr. Crooks—who has progressed Or 'Workhouse to Westminster—a clear man- ate to show the Government on what an insecure Pedestal it stands. Self-complacence is not proof agains b such a blow as the Woolwich men have tdhsalti, and the Government would do well to take ar,e 'Assam. to heart or an early disintegration is tistiredly its fate. We would just add one word tric°l3gratulation to the friends of Labour on the ,011:11Ph their cause has gained. Let Labour giwollYa walk hand in hand with progress and the inn,.ries of conquest will be experienced in an ever- -easing measure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>IttCEPTION AT RUNCORN. lb. S. Taylor h At Haan."</head>
        <p>IttCEPTION AT RUNCORN. lb. S. Taylor h At Haan."</p>
        <p>On • r.. Friday evening one of the most brilliant social :glaictions ever held in Runcorn took place at the Town flan when Mr. S. Taylor (chairman of the Urban kjouncil) was "At Home" to over 160 of his fellow ,townfolk. The various rooms at the Technical Institute 'QM been charmingly decorated, the Ladies Euterpean Orchestra being requisitioned to add to the attractive- Oess of the proceedings. The guests were received in the Lecture Hall of the Technical Institute Mr. S. Taylor and his daughter, Mrs. James Mason /lrith. The carriages began to arrive at 7.30, and after li.,ght refreshment had been handed round, the company tiourned to supper in an adjoining room, which was rWed in grand style by Messrs. dalt• and Capper, of erpool. The following was the menu : 110 GROSSES PIECES. P:rs Read ; Game Pie; Turkey k la Royal ; Roast To,,etc-ens aux Cressons Capons 6. in Bechamel ;Ox Cii-gknee ; Beef 6, la Chasseur ; Roast Pheasants; Ituleuerland Ham' Lobster Salad; Trifle' Charlotte Creams 6. la Celestine; Jellies 6, la. Reine ; Afrt'es ti la Francaise. L'hasiet ',upper the gentlemen retired to the Council Where Mr. S. Taylor's health wasproposed by Air.;el-1, ougal infelicitous terms. In a happy speech ble tnlior responded remarking that had it been possi- Would liave accommodated 500 instead of the 160 he say ti,„uave been much better pleased. It is needless to to jas7t Mr. Taylor made an ideal host and front first he proceedings were keenly enjoyed by all. gues-f. '4,111g possible to promote the happiness of the clas,,"B 'lad not been forgotten and a thoroughly high- artistic entertainment was the result. The Cair',,lnnle of music was as follows :—March "El ,° Sousa; selection "Gondoliers" Sullivan; (by 141 s; .Permission of Messrs. Chappells), Valse Blew, 4 Fla; melody in F, Rubinstein; selection "Merrie IN,Togoold" Ed. aerman; (by kind permission of Messrs. eaalvello and Co.), Pizzicato gavotte, Strauss; Morris Shepherd.s dance, Torch dance "Henry VIII." Ed. CO-'uli (by kind permission of Messrs. Novello and "Toreodor" Monckton; gavotte " Dans ge"; selection "Runaway Girl" Caryll; Minuet 'lochettes; Czardas "No. 6"Michiels. The gather- ," broke up at 11 o'clock, there being a general feeling thorough satisfaction. The following is the list of suests,</p>
        <p>LIST OF THE GUESTS. P 0 LIST and Mrs. R. H. Mc Lerman, Dr. &amp; Mrs. A virlYeet•man, Mr. M. Fisher, Mr. and Mrs. W. iswall, Mr. and Mrs. F. Rowland, Mr. W.and Miss ''llaw In , 0 Mr. J. J. Bake, Mr. and Mrs. H. ••'"richara, Mr. &amp; Mrs. G. H. Bellamy, Mr. and Mrs. G. 013nes, Mr. and Mrs. T. Harrison, Mr. and Mrs. G, Ikel d, Mr. J, H. R. H. Ireison Mr* and Mrs. E. Carroll, Mr. and Mrs. W. a. ey °tl ' 1..,, Mr. and Mrs. E. Raschen, Dr. and Mrs. "•,,ewitt, Mr. and Mrs. G. Timmins, Mr. and Mrs. C. „.„."ean, Mr. T.I Allen, Mr. and Mrs. P. aa•ndlev, Mr. W. Frith, Mr. E. Zilson, Mr. and Mrs. J. Beck, Mr. and Mrs. F. Qlsbrown, Mr. and Mrs. D. Chadwick, Mr. and Mrs.J. iv.alton, Mr. and Mrs. S. Beckett, Mr. S. C. htburn, Mr. and Mrs. Meadowcroft,Mr.&amp; Mrs. J. d • W Littler, Mr. J. tritcha*rd, Mr. J. and Miss Short, Mr. and Mrs. W. A Mr. and Mrs. F. A. Mount, Mr. and Mrs. E. 'alter, Mr. and Mrs. Sheard, Mr. &amp; Mrs. T. S.. bougall, Dr. F. Dear, Mr. and Mrs. J. A. iter, Mr. &amp; Mrs. M. Seatree. Mr. &amp; Mrs- G. lding. Mr, J. &amp; Miss Lee, Mr. and Mrs. H. -ri.,: rßhall, Mr. and Mrs. E. Gilchrist, Mr. jes, Mr. and Mrs. W. Alexander,Mr. and Mrs. c wrenson, Mr. T. A. Howard. Mr. and Mrs. C. uniliegham9 •Mr and Mrs. Frith, Mr. and Mrs. J. 1%1. 40 • Frith, Mr. and Mrs. G. I, reelield, Mr. and Mrs. J. • Downes, Mr. SC Mrs.F. F. ,u174.130,1V1r. and Mrs. S. Harrison. Mr. A. ~ °ll9 Mr. and Mrs. W. Westen,Mr. and Mrs. .I.H. Mr. &amp; Mrs. A. R. Lightburn. Mr. &amp; Mrs. A. lain', leg. Mr. and Mrs. W. Dobson, Mr. J. and Miss tek, Rev. C. FL Morris, Mr. R. Veillii, ali, Mr. and Mrs. P. .T. Unsworth, Mr. &amp; Mrs. J. Rev. and Mrs* A. and .Tones, Mrs. Tc.—"lsa Robinson, Mrs. ,virrrY. -Coppin, Rev. and Mrs. Lightfoot. Mrs. -ram Norman,Miss 0,43 ter, Rev. and Mrs. J. R. Handley, Mrs. T. , 11.,.,.- 2111.,,e, Rev. and Mrs. J. Stevenson, Miss IVr 'fly, aev. and Mrs. W. Cameron. Miss "rrie, Rev. and Mrs. S. Cornea, Mrs.</p>
