      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>FREEDOM OF ST. HELENS</head>
        <p>THE FREEDOM OF ST. HELENS</p>
        <p>interesting ceremony took nlace on Thursday at 'tlielens Town Hall, when Alderman .T. C. Gamble (,chairman of the Finance Committee) and T. Cook !chairman of the Gag C•mmittee) were made honorary Treemen of the borough, in recognition of their lengthy raenrrdyaluable services to the town. The resolutions con- distinction were enclosed in handsome I)e.,ckenentiße'd ti77:hairM:tYor (Councillor Diyzon-Nuttall) and made the prgentations. The Rneakers were Colonel Richard Pilkington, M. P.,' zald Alderman C. J. Bishop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A MONEY-LENDING CASE AT RIINCOILN,</p>
        <p>Immediately before the rising of the Nisi Prins Court at Chester Assizes on Tuesday evening the case of Annie Kenwright v. Mather was called on. The plaintiff, a widow, deposed to having lent £lOO to the defendant, a schoolmaster of Runcorn, to enable him to enter Dur- ham University. Mrs. Kenwright swore to the lending of the money-80 of her own savings and £2O procured for the purpose elsewhere. Her daughter also deposed to fetching the £BO from the hank.—There was no re- sponse for the defendant, and the learned judge at once gave judgment for the full amount claimed, with costs, and interest at the rate of five per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>GIS EXHIBITION AND COOKERY LECTURES.</head>
        <p>GIS EXHIBITION AND COOKERY LECTURES.</p>
        <p>During the last week a gks stove cooking exhibition has been held in the Bethesda Hall, Vicar-street, for the purpose of bringing prominently before the public the" Wilson Gas Cookers," which can be procured from the Runcorn Gas Company. Each afternoon lectures have been givenby Mrs. C. F. Pitcher, Principal of the Streatham School of Cookery. The chief advantage of the " Cooker " is its comparative cheapness over fuel in the cooking of food, its cleanliness and its saving of time. There have been large audiences each afternoon to witness the Cookers" at work. THE CONCEALMENT OF BIRTH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Chester Assizes on Monday before Mr. Justice Bruce, Margaret Gough (23),charwoman pleaded guilty to endeavouring to conceal the dead body of a child at Runcorn on the 12th January. Mr. R. V. Banks proses- cuted.—The Judge said this was not a very bad case, because, prisoner, when accused, admitted immediately that she had thrown the child's body in the canal.—Mr. Bankes said he understood there were people willing to help her.—His Lordship said he had received a com- munication from them.—Prisoner was bound over in her own recognizances to come up for judgment, and Mr. Bankes stated that Miss Wright, of the Girls' Rescue Home, Chester, had undertaken to do what she could for her. (lira ,s 1</p>
        <p>" Y " BRANCrt OF THE RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPER-</p>
        <p>ANCE ASSOCI9.TION.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above was held on Wednesday in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom which WaS kindly lent for the occasion. Mrs. Baker presided over a very good attendance of members and friends. " Minute " speeches were introduced at this meeting the following members contributing :—Mrs. T. Simkin and the Misses Lowe, Bell, Harrop, Eyre and Stubbs. A very interesting feature was a dialogue en- titled " Children of To-morrow," which was given by Miss Marples and Miss Hooper of Birkenhead. Mrs. Mack accompanied and the musical part of the pro- gramme consisted of songs, etc., rendered by the Misses Harrop and Clare. The singing of the Temperance Doxology brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>REMAR.KABLE AGREEMENT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>REMAR.KABLE AGREEMENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Court, on Wednesday, before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, Samuel Claff, furniture dealer, 285, Bury New-road, Manchester, sued Harry Temple, Irwell-lane, Runcorn, for £lB 4s. 6d. under an agreement. Mr. Browne, Warrington, was for the plaintiff, and Mr. Wardle (Burton and Wardle) for the defendant. Mr. Browne said the defendant's son, Sydney, had been in Mr. ClA's employ for many years. About 2i years ago he became wrong in his accounts to the extent of 15. The defendant pleaded that it was pure carelessness, and if Mr.. Claff would take back his son he would become responsible for the deficiency. He did so, and also signed an undertaking to be responsible for any further deficiency. The son then went as a traveller to Chester, and later to Earlestown. It was lately found that he was over -Cl5 wrong in his accounts, two watches were missing, and he would not return his collecting book,making it impossible for Mr. Claff to trace about .7;100. • Mr. Claff corroborated this state- ment. Mr. Browne said his client had offered to take ten guineas and costs. The defendant now agreed to pay this, and his Honour gave a verdict accordingly, the amount to be paid at 15s per month,or lOs per month if the collectin book were returned in 21 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A. CORRESPONDENT.'</p>
        <p>At the iuljour'ilod moetiug of the Urban Council it was unanimously resolved to levy a penny rate for the upkeep of the Technical Institute. The conference at Crewe with the County Council dispelled any fear that that body would try to seize the Institute or endeavour to control any portion of the penny rate. The County Council intend to let the Runcorn Urban Council have full control of the schools, thanks mainly to the clause that was specially introduced with the Education Act by the cautions of Sir John T. Brunner.</p>
        <p>Mr. Posnett voiced the opinion that the County Council had not definitely pledged itself to anything and contended it was an unwise policy to vote the pro- ceeds of a penny rate. As Mr. Posnett emphasized his opinions in no uncertain manner, he was promptly brought to book by Mr. E. Imbaon, who carried the Council with him so that there was practically a unanimous vote that the rate be levied.</p>
        <p>As Mr. Posnett truly said Mr. unison appears to be the stumbling block over which the Hospital Committee, with its chairman, are continually falling. At the ad- journed Council meeting Mr. Posnett proposed that the Council form a committee to work with the Cottage Hospital Committee for the completion of the plans. Mr. Imison pointed out, however, that the Council might, if they took such a step be responsible for ex- penses in furnishing the hospital and the fear was so strong that the Council, in spite of protests that there was sufficient money to furnish practically resolved to have nothing to do with the Hospital until it was made over to them" bag and baggage." If the Council had only displayed this Scot-like cautiousness with other transactions how much money would the ratepayers have been saved?</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Water question still worries the Rural Council, which obviously intends to do something des- perate ere long. There was quite a ripple of merri- ment in the Board room—in view of the price of the mains—when Mr. Chadwick explained that the pressure at some of the houses in Weston was so bad that the taps were always turned on to catch whatever water the people could get.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Maryport visited Runcorn on Saturday and were given a gruelling they will not forget in a hurry. It was a poor game and the least said about it the better. In the second half the Linnets simply threw away the chances and gave the visitors openings which any other team would have seized to the discomforture of the opponents. The game would have been much better— froll a spectators point of view—if Runcorn could have played an A team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>COLLIERY DISASTER AT ~ COLLINS GREEN.</head>
        <p>COLLIERY DISASTER AT ~ COLLINS GREEN.</p>
        <p>Two Men Killed.</p>
        <p>A serious colliery disaster happened at the Collins Green Colliery, near St Helens on Wednesday morning when two men were killed, and two youths seriously in- jured. The men were Patrick Lawler, of 33 Watery-lane Sutton, St Helens, and Patrick Glynn, of Back Market street, Earlestown. The injured youths are James Bothwell, aged fourteen, of Brunswich-street: Earlestown and Thomas Bradley, of Birch-street, Newton Common. As far as can be ascertained the steel wire haulage rope broke as a number of tubs were going down a steep declivity in the yard mine, and before the men had any opportunity of getting clear the tubs were on the top of them. Patrick Lawler, who was a balancer, twenty-one years of age, was found under the tubs quite dead, whilst Patrick Glynn, who was a day wageman, twenty-four years old, was found on the top of the waggons. Glynn is married and leaves a widow and two children. Both men had received fearful injuries about the head. There bodies were removed to the Pear Tree Inn, Collins Green, to await the inquest. . The injured lads were removed home. Their injuries are of a serious character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>FRODSHAM RURAL DEANERY. Conference at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM RURAL DEANERY. Conference at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Bishop of Chester on Church Discipline.</p>
        <p>At the half-yearly conference of the rural deanery of Frodsham, on Thursday afternoon, in the Church House, Runcorn, the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, the rural dean, presided. After routine business. the Rev. F. A. Screeton, vicar of Latchford (Warrington), delivered an address on "The Parson's Freehold," which he traced historically and constitutionally. The Bishop of Chester took part in the discussion,and said there was no other set of men who held such an entrenched position as the clergy, and yet there were other professions without so much security of tenure who did their duty extremely well. He realised that there was a great deal of difficulty attached to the pre- sent condition of things, and far be it from him to pro- pose offhand a solution of a state of affairs which was bound up so much with history and constitutional But he must confess that when he instituted a promising young man into a living—into practically an entrenched position—he could not help feeling he was doing him a doubtful kindness. He was placed in a position where he could say "I am vicar," and within certain limits he could defy the congregation and the bishop and all other authorities. It was a fair question to consider how far he freeholds might be modified without injuring the easonable security of the parson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Examiner Friday, March 13, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Friday, March 13, 1903.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. G. Knowles in the chair.—Present: Messrs. C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw A. H. Talbot, E. Clarke, J. Stokes,J. Whitlow, T. Dar- lington T. Baxter, J. M. Williams, T. Wright, W. H. Bradford, J. T. Collier, S. Woodward, G. Pilling, S. Horton E. Bretherton J. Edwards, J. Spruce, A. Eaton, J. B. Chadwick, J. Motinfield, R. Bill ington, with Messrs. W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), A. Hulse, (deputy assis- tant) Dr. Adams (medical officer) J. Swinton and W. Diggle (sutveyors). SE,ITS FOR CONS UMPTFVE XATIENTS. Dr. Wood, of the Liverpool Sanatorium, Kingswood, wrote that in deference to the Council's decision he had not placed any seats in the Toads. It was true he had the permission of the local tenants, and landowners, to place a few seats in the banks of their hedges. They had allowed him to excavate the banks and place seats in the recess so that they did not project on the road in the slightest. The Clerk said he had sent a copy of the letter to Mr. Diggle. Mr. Digglesaid that ifthe seats wereplaced in the side of the cop in a slanting direction, he did not see any great danger in allowing the seats to be there only the Council had decided not to allow them to be erected. Mr. Baxter: Does the front of the seat project above the bottom of the cop? Mr. Diggle: Practically it rests on the foot of the cop. The Chairman: Then there is no obstruction.</p>
        <p>Horton was of opinion that the Council should not go back on its previous decision not to allow seats to be placed on the road for consumptive patients. It was a menace to the public health to allow them to sit on seats which might afterwards be occupied by healthy people. Mr. Woodward thought Mr. Horton was entirely mis- taken in supposing that the seats would engender disease. If the seats were erected not on the road but on other property the Council had nothing to do with it. He suggested a committee be formed to consider the question. Mr. Wright stated he had seen the place and there was no obstruction. It would be a pity not to allow the poor people to sit down. If there were no seats they would sit on the grass so that any probable danger would just be the same. Resolved that Messrs. Baxter, Horton, Bright and Talbot be appointed to consider the question and re- port to the Council.</p>
        <p>NVALTO.N ARCHES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Walton Inferior Parish Cannon wrote calling attention to the height of Walton Arches over the road. The height of the iron arch was only thirteen feet four inches and the stone arch thirteen feet nine inches. The Act stipulated for a clear and open space under every arch of not less than fifteen feet a height of not less than sixteen feetfrom the surface of the river tol the centre of the arch. The Council's attention had been called to the dangerous condition of the fence along the old canal but nothing had yet been done. .</p>
        <p>After considerable discussion on the above question it was resolved that the clerk make inquiry as to the exact wording of the Act relating to the height of such arches and to find out whether there was any special agreement between the Highway authority of that time and the railway company, the bridge having been erected fifty years.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WATER. Iq lA. COMPLAINT FROM WESTON. IMr. A. Rosenburg, of Mersey View, Weston Point, wrote on March sth, to the Chairman of the Rural District Council complaining of the inadequacy of the water supply for domestic purposes to his cottages at "Beaconsfield Views "and" Clarks Terrace ", Weston Point. He said that for some time he had been in com- munication with the Runcorn Urban District Council about the insufficient supply, and they had informed him that the supply pipes were corroded, owing to the hardness of the water, but he contended that the Urban Council's mains were at fault being so corroded as to diminish the pressure considerably, and said that he had proved this fact in the presence of the Urban Council's engineer. Some of the supply pipes were leaden ones, and all had been examined. If the force were sufficient the cottages would receive a sufficient quantity for all purposes. He trusted thqt that Rural Council would do something to force the Urban District Council to have the mains examined in the presence of 'their representatives. The Clerk said that bearing on the above question a letter had been received from the Urban District Coun- cil saying that the Water Committee had appointed a sub-committee with regard to the District Council's inquiry as to the state of the mains. The Chairman: We're getting on. (Laughter.) _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Woodward : Yes. - Mr. Chadwick said that Mr. Rosenbnrg was quite right. There was absolutely no pressure there at all. There should be at least 100 pounds pressure on the pipes judging by his own works. The other day he saw a three inch pipe opened and water did not spout one inch high. The taps at some of the houses were left on all night to catch what water they could. Mr. Woodward: This is very interesting information in view of the price that has been asked for the mains. (Laughter.) Mr. Chadwick: The pipes have been down 35 years and they must be in a bad condition. It was resolved that a copy of Mr.fßosenburg'S letter be sent to the Urban Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>RE-APPOINTMENT OF DR. ADAMS.</head>
        <p>RE-APPOINTMENT OF DR. ADAMS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Messrs. Wright and Clarke it was resolved that Dr. Adams be re-appointed medical officer at a salary of £l5O.</p>
        <p>FINANCE,</p>
        <p>The statement showed that the balance at last meet- ing was £ll6 12s. 6d; receipts £575185. ; cheques drawn that day, £279 4s. 10d; balance £413 ss. Bd. On the motion of Mr. Wright the statement was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>THELWALL NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE THELWALL NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer stated that the report of Pro- fessor De La Pienne would not be ready until the next Council meeting, owing to the inclusion of some extraneous material.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>SMALL-PDX AT HELSBY.</head>
        <p>SMALL-PDX AT HELSBY.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported that there was a case of small-pox at Helsbv. All the contacts had beenvaccina- ted, and the bedding and clothing destroyed. About ninety of the workmen had also been vaccinated at the Helsby Wire Works, and the others would also be vaccinated he presumed. Mr. Woodward: The local doctor will be having a good day. (Laughter.) Mr. Collier said that the vaccination was being done at the men's own expense, and as it would cost about Xl5O to the local authority had they done it, the thanks .of the Council were due to them.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Wright, the Clerk was instruc- ted to send the thanks of the Council to the workmen of the Helsby Wire Works.</p>
        <p>THE MAINTENANCE OP THE ROADS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter introduced theabove question by remark- ing that he did not think it was necessary for two men to be employed together to look after the road. The County Council had dispensed with men working in couples andhe thought the Rural Council could do the same. They bad been given to understand that with a steam roller fewer men would be required. On the suggestion of Mr. Chadwick the matter was de- ferred to the next meeting of the Highway Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ESTIMATES.</head>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright submitted a report as to the probable balance in favour of or against each parish at the end of the current half year ending. Lady Day 1903 and an esti- mate of the amount of contributions required front each parish during the half year ending Michaelmas 1903. The following items were given :—Total capital for current half year £4,839, probable expenditure £3,891, balance at the end of the half year £948, probable ex- penditure during ensuing half year £1,586, total amount required from the overseers £3,636; assessable value of the district £157,630. A rate of sid. in the pound would produce £3,608, and it was recommended that a call for £3,636, being an increase of a half-penny in the pound on the call for the corresponding period last year be made on the overseers. The contributions for each of the parishes was as follows :—Acton Grange, £B6; Alvanley £37; Antrobus £59; Appleton £135; Aston £102; Acton Grange £11; Bartington £l2; Budworth £46; Clifton £75; Crowley £3O; Daresbury £24; Dutton £180; Frodsham £280; Frodshani Lordship £180; Grappenhall £160; Halton £103; Hatton £36 Helsby £208; Keckwick £63; Kingsley £120; Kings wood £33; Latchford (Without) £6O; Morley £35 Moore £99; Newton-by-Daresbury £9; Newton-by Frodsham £2O; Norley £B9; Norton £127; Preston o'-th'-Hill £B2; Sevenoaks £2Ol Stockham £18; Stock ton Heath £235; Stretton £4B; Sutton £150; Thelwal £110; Walton Inferior £117; Weston £299; Whitley Inferior £35; Whitley Superior £43; total £3,636. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RUNCORN APPRENTICE LOST AT SEA.</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN APPRENTICE LOST AT SEA.</p>
        <p>CORK, THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>The master of the four-masted ship gorteviot, .mhich has arrived in Cork harbour, reports that on the 2nd inst., an apprentice named Thomas Ormsby, a native of Runcorn, was lost overboard. Captain Kidd states that Ormsby, who was very popular on board, was only on his first voyage. The young fellow was full of courage and gave every promise of becoming a first-class mariner. He had learned as much seamanship during his short time at sea as others would in three voyages. On the night of the 2nd inst, it was coming on to blow a gale, and several hands went aloft to furl sail. Young Ormsby with others was on the foretop gallant yard. One of the hands on the yard was attracted to what he thought was a light from the place where Ormsby was, and looking in the direction be observed that the boy was gone. The seaman went down on deck and reported the occurence at once. One man on deck said he had heard a splash, which must have been caused by the boy's fall into the sea from a height of 130 feet. All the time the vessel was going at the rate of 13 knots an hour, and the night was intensely dark, with a big sea running, so that it was found to be impossible to make any effort to rescue the unfortunate apprentise. All on board, from the Captain down, were fond of the boy, and his untimely death caused a feeling of deep regret. The occurrence took place at this side of the Western Islands. The deceased was a son of Dr. Ormsby, of IRuncorn, and the news of the sad occurrence was re- ceived with regret in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above authority was held on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse under the presi- dency of Mr. J. Stokes. Present :—Messrs. A. R. Norman (vice chairman), S.Taylor, T. Wood, J. Douglas, the members of the Rural District Council with Messrs. W. G. Houghton (assistant clerk) and A. E. Hulse (deputy assistant), J. Cannah (master), T. Cooke, W. R. DePrez and T.Riley (relieving officers.)</p>
        <p>THE PAYMENT OF GUARDIANS ATTENDLNG POOR LAW</p>
        <p>.CONF E RE= E.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote intimating that they had intended to consolidate the orders issued under the Poor Law Conferences Act, 1883 empowering Guardians to pay the reasonable expenses incurred in attending conferences of Guardians held to discuss any matter connected with their duties. The Clerk said that the order removed the former restrictions which limited the conferences to be held within one hundred miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>EMIGRATION TO CANADA.</head>
        <p>EMIGRATION TO CANADA.</p>
        <p>A circular was received from.the Local Government Board drawing attention to the fact that there had been a considerable diminution in the number of children emigratedto Canada by Boards of Guardians. In 1893 the number was 360 compared with 141 last year. The Board wished the Guardians to give the matter atten- tion with a view to the emigration of suitable children.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the cost o'f.' sending a cliild to Canada was £l5.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We have done nothing of that kind yet. The Clerk: No. The circular was referred to the Visiting Committee.</p>
        <p>IMBECILES IN WORKHOUSES.</p>
        <p>The following resolution was read whichhaAl been for- warded by the Chelmsford Union having been passed by them :—" That as the presence of imbeciles and epileptics in workhouses is a cause of great diS-comfort to other inmates and is at the same time far from being benefi- cial to themselves the Local Government Board be urged to take steps for the removal into separate Institutions and that a letter be sent to every Union in the Kingdom asking them to co-operate in the matter. Mr. Williams moved that the Board support the reso- lution. There had been an imbecile in the Home some time ago and was a source of considerable annoyance to the other inmates. It wa,s high time that the Government took the matter in hand.</p>
        <p>Dr. Douglas seconded and the resolution was passed.</p>
        <p>THE ROLTRKE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from Dr. Ga—sta- ldi asking the Guardians to adopt the Rourke children for the purpose ofPeing sent to Roman Catholic Institutions.—A further letter was received asking the matter to stand over as he could not attend the next Guardians meeting. The Clerk stated that in reply lie had written to Dr. Gastaldi informing him that the Board had decided at its last meeting to adopt the children for a month. On the motion of Mr. Woodward the matter was de- ferred for a fortnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>SMALL-PDX AT PENTASAPII.</head>
        <p>SMALL-PDX AT PENTASAPII.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Domestic Training School, Pentasaph, stating that in consequence of the small-pox epidemic the children had to be vaccinated, and some would be chargeable to the Union. A discussion ensued, Mr. Woodward pointing out that it was the duty of the public vaccinator at Holy- well to vaccinate the children and that the cost was not chargeable to the Runcorn Union. On the motion of Mr. Baxter it was resolved that the clerk write to the Orphanage stating that the Board had no objection to the children being vaccinated provided it be done to the cost of the district where the children lived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>VACCINATION OF VAGRA.NTS.</head>
        <p>THE VACCINATION OF VAGRA.NTS.</p>
        <p>• A copy of petition was sent by the do—nileton Union re the compulsory vaccination of vagrants. The Union believed that the vaccination of vagrants passing through the casual wards would reduce the number of small-pox patients, and prayed the House of Commons to legislate on the subject. The Clerk pointed out that at the last meeting the Board had resolved to support the memorial, and on the motion of Mr. Williams the seal of the Union was affixed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CLERK'S REMUNERATION.</head>
        <p>THE CLERK'S REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward stated that at a meetingof the Assess- ment Committee a payment of £lOO was recommended to the Clerk for his services, it being the same as in former years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EDUCATION RATE</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION RATE</p>
        <p>A letterwas read by Mr. Potts. clerk to the County Cqpncil, informing the Board that the Co mty Council had petitioned the Board of Education to postpone the dates fixing the County Educational area until the let July. If granted thd amount required would necessi- tate a rate of at least 2d. in the R. and would, in all pro- bability, be levied at the meeting of the County Council to be kheld in May. An additional rate ought also be necessary for higher education. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. WOodwar‘d then submitted a report giving the estimated balances in favour of the several parishes of the Union at the end of the half Tear, Lady day 1903, and also an estimate (calculated ou the present assess- able value) of the amount of contributions required for the next half-year ending Michtelmas. Total capital for current half-year £7,712, probable expenditure £5,841 balance at Lady day .21871. Estimated expenditure to Michmlmas ; . Common charges £3,936, County rate £3,211 Elementary education £1,729, contin,gency and balance £1,231, total £10,107 Deducting the Lady Day balance, the amount required for the overseers would be £8,236, which would be met by a rate of 9d. in the 2 on the assessable value of £213,144; producing £7,992. This rate is an increase of lid. in the £ on the corresponding period of last year, the increase being caused by the provision of funds to meet the precept of the Cheshire County Council under the new Education Act on account of Elementary Edu- cation. This precept would be issued in May next. The amount of the several contributions from each parish in the Union was as follows :—Acton Grange £139, Alvanley £62, Antrobus £9B, Appleton £220, Aston £169, Acton Grange £lB, Bartington £23, Budworths £7B, Clifton £l3O, Crowley £49,Daresbury £4O, Dutton £290, Frodsham £460, Frodsham Lordship £290, Grappenhall £275, Halton £lBl, Hatton £6l, Helsby £319, Keckwick £124, Kingsley £2OO, Kingswood £s3,Latchford (without) £99; Morley £6l: Moore £170; Newton-by-Daresbnry £4B ;.Newton-by-Frodsham £33; Norley £133; Norton £215; Preston-o'th'-Hill £133; Runcorn £2,196; Seven- oaks £35; Stockham £26;- Stockton Heath £386; Stretton £72; Sutton £241; Thelwall £180; Walton Inferior £185; WaltoroSuperior £80; Weston £499; Whitley Inferior £6l; Whitley Superior £6B; total £8,236.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: The increase is caused by the Educa- tion Act coming into force.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodwatla: Yes. But it will not be necessary to levy on behalf of school attendance, which amounts to nearly one penny in the se so that there is a set off. le VAGRANTS IN QUARANTINE. The Clerk said that Dr. Adams had given instructions to keep all vagrants under observ.ion for 14 days .be- fore coming into the house proper. The Chairman: A very wise precaution. THE STATE OF THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Williams said that the Visiting Committee had made a tour of the house and that everything had been done satisfactorily. There were 16 or 18 less in the house than last year. Tramps were less numerous.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN DISTRICT INFANT SCHOOL The annual concert given by the infants of the Eaxlestown District Schools was held in the Mission Hall Leigh-street on Monday evening. The Hall was packed and a very good programme was submitted. The children had been carefully trained by the teachers and performed their various parts in an excellent 'manner. The programme opened with a chorus by the children 'The dear old home songs ". A solo "Birdie from the west" was nicely sung by Miss Lilian Burke. Kinder- garten games " Bonnie Boat "and" merry boys and merry maidens "were played by theichildren in a very lively manner. Miks Pattie Banks contributed two songs " The old Sock" and "The Quakers Daughter" and Miss Molyneux sang "Tiny feet " and " Nobody else". The lancers dance by. light couples reflected great credit on the teachers who had trained the children as also did an action song "Postman "by six boys. A recitation" Some lovers of Rain" was given by five girls and one boy. A short sketch by six boys and six girls entitled " Giant slayers "caused much merriment. A chorus "Good night "by the children brought the entertainment to a close. During the interval the chairman(the Rev. T. R. Matthews) stated that the proceeds were to be devoted to the forthcoming bazaar in aid of the church competition.</p>
        <p>FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warringion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for BEST BOOTS in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALP TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt..</p>
        <p>FOR CLASS AND /Cll INA of every description I ry .T. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Irenmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>"L 0 T II IIA. N"</p>
        <p>SHO RTB RE AD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT. Annual Report Ofthe Medical Officer.</head>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT. Annual Report Ofthe Medical Officer.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams, the Medical Officer of Health for the Run- corn Rural District, has just issued his report for the year 1902. The report is a very lengthy one, among the particulars given being the following :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>POPUL A.TION.</head>
        <p>POPUL A.TION.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer estimates the population of the district in the middle of 1902 to be 23,256.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS AND DEATHS.-INTERESTING: COMPARISONS.</p>
        <p>During the year ending December 31st last there were 644 births and 320 deaths registe.ted in the district, as compared with an average for the previous ten years of 651 births and 379 deaths. Of the 644 births, 322 were males and 322 females, the number of each sex, thus being exactly equal. The previous year there was only a difference of 2 between the sexes, and the year before there was only a difference of one. The birth-rate for the whole district during 1902 was 271 per 1,000 per annum. The birth-rate is the highest occuring in any year since 1896.</p>
        <p>DISEISES.</p>
        <p>No cases of small-pox were notified in 1902. Diph- theria seems almost to have disappeared, whilst measles 1111,4 only been known in scattered cases. Only two cases of scarlet fever have occurred since October. THE QUALITY ON RUNCORN WATER.</p>
        <p>Dr Adams gives particulars of the extension of the water supply to the various parishes in the district dur- ing the year. Alluding to the quality of water supplied by the Runcorn Urban District Council to the town- ships of Halton and Weston, including Weston Point, he states:—"Bacteriological analysis of this waterhave not given results satisfactory to your Council, although the Urban District Council appear to be satisfied with the results of analysis performed on their behalf, thus leaving the question of a proper supply to these two townships in an uncertain and indecisive condition. During the year I suggested that duplicate samples be submitted by the Urban and the Rural Councils for bacteriological analysis, but this suggestion was not adopted. I think this matter should not be left where it is, as it is at present unsatisfactory alike to both the Urban and the District Councils, as well as to the users of the water in that portion of the district supplied by it."</p>
        <p>SEWAGE ARRANGEMITS.</p>
        <p>, Dr. Adams makes some instinctive remarks in refer- ence to the sewage arrangements, and specially draws the Council's attention to the great strides recently made in various methods of sewage purification. He impresses upon the Council the necessity for adopting such measures wherever practicable in new undertakings.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW HOSPITAL. 'SZReferring to the prevention of infectious diseases the medical officer statv that he is glad to report the Council are now within sight of the new hospital for infectious diseases. A site has been purchased in Dutton, the sanction of the Local Government Board obtained, with borrowing powers, an architect's plans have been adopted, after competition, and it only now remains to advertise for tenders, and to commence the work, after obtaining the necessary financial aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>THELWALL NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE THELWALL NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams further: reports that during 1902 works of sewage re-construction have been undertaken with refer- ence to the "alleged tannery nuisance," and the Council has requested the Director of the Public Health Labora- tory, Owen's College, to examine and report upon the allegations made against the Cliffe-lane Tannery and against your Council. His (Dr. Adams') report will not be presented until early in 1903. He therefore refrains from any further observations. One case of typhoid ever occurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Presentation.</p>
        <p>On Monday a tea party and presentation took place at the Mersey Mission in connection with the Mother's meeting. It was held on the occasion of Mrs. Griffiths, one of the members of the Mission leaving England for Queensland. Upwards of GO sat down to an excellent tea including the Misses Wingfield Digby. After tea Mr. Shaw said he was pleased to see such a good number present, and as one of their members was leaving them they thought it would be a good thing to make a small present as a token of their respect. He then called upon Miss Wingfield Digby to make the 'presentation. Miss Digby then presented a ladies' companion to Mrs. Griffiths, together with the following testimonial : "Presented by Miss Wingfield .Digby on behalf of the members of the mother's meeting to Mrs. J. Griffiths on her leaving England as a small token of their esteem and best wishes for her future welfare in her new home in a far off land." Mr. Shaw, missioner, thanked the members on her behalf. The rest of the evening was spent in parlour games. ' Concert.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, the special concert which was given by the single ladies in aid of new chairs for the Institute, was well patronized. A splendid programme was given several of the items being encored. At the close votes of thanks were passed to Miss B. Johnson, accompanist, and Miss Bate for presiding. Mr. Shaw also proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the single ladies for the excellent programme they had provided, this was seconded by Mr Mush and carried with great applause. The singing of the National anthem brought a very pleasant evening to t close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Annual Report of the Institute.</head>
        <p>Annual Report of the Institute.</p>
        <p>The annual report presented by Mr. W. Shaw, missioner, is as follows :—ln presenting my annual report for 1902, I am thankful to say the work in all its branches in this outport becomes of more marked in- terest every year, a number of large grain ships having been paid off in Runcorn. lam received heartily by the masters and men of these ships, and of the small coast- ing vessels who have known me and my work among them for the last 27 years, and I am able to have a hearty shake of the hand with them and welcome them once more. Mr. Tonks, the Ttistitut3 keeper, is always willing to help in making them quite at home when they enter the Church or Institute. Services in the Mission Church are very well attended in the evening. It is very encouraging all the year through. The average is about 150 persons' including sailors' families and mission workers. The most we have had was on October 12th, 388 present, and the services are much appreciated by the sailors and masters who attend. Nine men and women belonging to the mission were prepared for confirmation; seven were confirmed at Runcorn,and one subsequently at Newton-le-Willows. On Easter Sunday 30 from the mission attended the Holy Com- munion at the Parish Church, and those who were just confirmed received the Holy Communion for the first time. I am sorry to say that the sailors do not come forward as T could wish to the Holy Communion. We have an average of 12 communicants a month, but most ,of them are workers. The Institute is being better attended now than ever, and proves a great boon to the men, who enjoy the games, draughts, dominoes, ping pong, etc. lam sorry to say our bagatelle table is worn out, we should have another one in its place. The sailors and watermen seem very grateful for the sailor parties in the summer and are greatly delighted. Each time they arrive in Runcorn they inquire about the Challenge Cup which they helped to win at the tugs-of-war. There is no doubt most of the men will never forget the kindness they have received from the good people who have entertained them. Sunday school work has been very encouraging. The men's and women's bible classes have increased in numbers, and the average attendance of the whole school is 220. For regular attendance and good conduct 32 bibles and prayer books, at a cost, of one shilling each, were presented during the year, the cost of which was paid by the children and workers of the Sunday School. It is pleasing to note that 26 children made the possible number of attendances, 104 times, in the year. The Mother's Meeting and Scripture Union, which was commenced after the Special Mission by the Rev. S. H. Wingfield-Digbv and Miss Wingfield- Digby is going on successfully. It wouldtake too long to give details of the Anniversary Services, Whitsun- tide and Christmas Treats, Excursions to Liverpool and Newlßrighton, and up the Canal, all of which were very successful. On Good Triday at the Lantern Service, we had 300 people and children present. The special Services held in Holy Week were fairly attended A very important branch of the work is the visiting of sailors and boatmen in their vessels and at their homes in sickness. On the large grain ships from San Fran- cisco, Tacoma and Portland (Oregon), I am well received when I mention .the names of the Chaplains whom the men have known at these several ports, and am able to advise and help them. I have received several letters from them after theyhave arrived home, thanking me for my help to them during their stay in Runcorn. In March three men gave me £73 to take care of them until they left Runcorn, several who had been sick on board their vessels seem very grateful for the , help I was able to give them. One sailor lad in the Union. Hospital seemed very grateful to me for my visits and help to him. The weekly concerts, through the kindness of many friends of sailors we are able to carry on, are much appreciated. and the men turn up in good numbers. The recreation geld and the pleasure ground in the summer months are a great attraction, and an i advantage to the Mission. It s quite amusing to see theway in which the masters and men of the coasting vessels enjoy themselves on the skittle alley. lam more than grateful for the great help given me by my kind friends, both ladies and gentlemen, Sunday school teachers (17 in number), the choir (23 in number), and those who have helped at the weekly concerts, &amp;c., also those who have given muff ers and funds for the treats at Chrtstmas and Whitsuntide. My special thanks also are. due to Messrs. W. Shaw, and J. Stokes, Lay Readers, R. Shaw, Organist, J: K. Digges, hon. sec., and the Misses Hayes, of Frodsham.—Number of letters written by seamen' for the year 1902, 167 ; parcels of books given to seamen, 129. Total subscriptions, £23 17s. 10d. BREACH OF. THIS CHILD MESSENGER ACT AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at the Newton Petty Sessions, Bernard Fagan, of Earlestown, pleaded guilty to having senthis daughter, under the age of fourteen years, to the Royal Hotel, Earlestown, for three pints of ale and stout. Defendant was fined Zis, and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWFON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWFON.</p>
        <p>ADJOURNED LICENSING SESSIONS AT NEWTON-L E- MMA WS.</p>
        <p>At these sessions, held on Thursdly, there was only one deferred licence, the Limerick Inn, Burtonwood, to be dealt with. The police having withdrawn the objec- tion, the licence was granted. P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the P.S.A. was held iu the Co-operative Hall, Earle-street, Earlestown on Sunday last. There was a good attendance presided over by Mr. Potter. Several musical selections were rendered by the Haydock Band. Solos were contributed by Miss Allan,and Miss B. Smith was the reciter. Miss Williamson accompanied at the piano and Mr. E. Smith presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>We understand that the contest for Crow-lane Ward is likely to be a three-cornered one. Mr. J. Fitzgerald is retiring, and those who will probibly seek to step into the vacancy are Mr. A. Evett, Market-street, Mr. P. Naylor, Earle-street, and Mr. W. Beatty,Earle-street. Town Hall Ward and Viaduct Ward will in all proba- bility be uncontested, but there ought to be contests in the Wargrave and Newton Wards.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION CONSERVATIVE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above was held at the Con- servative Club Earlestown on Monday evening. Mr. C. B. F.l3orron occupied the chair. The whole of the committee and officials were re-elected ea bloc. An address was given by Mr. F. W; Deakin. The following resolution was passed, viz., resolved that this meeting desires to express its continued confidence in the Right Hon. A. J. Balfour, M.P., and his colleagues being fully convinced that the domestic legislation passed by them is calculated to promote the best interests of all classes of his Majesty's subjects and also their foreign and colonial policies are worthy of the support of all those who desire the security and prosperity of the British Empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>COLLIERY OFFENCES Ail' HAYDOCK.</head>
        <p>COLLIERY OFFENCES Ail' HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>On Thursday several charges concerning offences com- mitted at the Wood Pit, Ha,ydock, belonging to Messrs. R. Evans and Co., Limited, wore heard at Newton Petty Sessions. John Roberts was fined 20s. and costs for taking a box of matches; into the pit.-31ich2e1 Com- mody descended the shaft with a quantity of unprotected explosive, and was ordered to pay a similar fine.— Ephraim Williams had in his possession a tobacco pipe, and Dennis Burke rode down the pit in a cage contain- ing working tools, and both were fined ss. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SHociirsa CASE OF CRUELTY TO CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>SHociirsa CASE OF CRUELTY TO CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>A shockinr, case of cruelty to children by neglect came before the Newton-le-Willows Bench on Thursday morning, when the National Society prosecuted James and Ellen Jolley,man and wife, of 9 Brick-street, Earles- town, for the neglect of their children.—lnspector Hackett said the man was a moulder, who earned .£2 4s. a week, but the children and the house were in i terrible condition. The children were more like wild animals thab human beings, and one of them, on one of his visits clawed at witness like a cat. The children were very verminous, and in a shockingly dirty condition, though on one occasion he noticed that some improvement had taken place, but things lapsed into the same fifthy con- dition.—The defendants promised the Bench to stop their drinking habits, and to provide properly for the children, on which understanding the case was adjourned for a month. ANNUAL VISIT OF MR. HARRY BuxTox's CIIOIR.</p>
        <p>Earlestown's favourite entertainer, Mr. Harry Bux- ton with his excellent varied combination will visit the Town Hall, Earlestown on Tuesday next, and will occupy the Hall for seven nights. Mr. Buxton needs no eulogy from us, his fame being sufficient to warrant a first class entertainment, free from any semblance of vulgarity, and he is sure of Satisfactory audiences on each night of his stay. His electric animated photo- graphy Is second to none in the kingdom, and he has spared no expense to put before the public an entertain-. ment in every way of the,highest class. There are many new features the most nOtable, being the "Fantastical trip to the Moon," a picture 1,000 feet long. Of enter- tainers he has plenty, and a night of thorough enjoy- ment is assured. BACHELOR'S TEA. AND CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The fifth annual tea -nd concert arranged by the Baptist Bachelors was held in the schoolroom on Thurs- day evening. About 230 sat dowi .to tea, which was served at 6.30, the+achelors attending to all details, cutting up, itc. Fines were imposed as in previous years, and much amusement was caused in the detecting of those who made themselves liable. The concert was held in the schoolroom at 8 o'clock. The hall was well filled, and an excellent programme was submitted. The company of Bachelors opened with a chorus "Monarch of the Foam," followed by a very good rendering of "Good Company," by Mr. Jack Sephton. A humorous duet " The Twin," by Messrs. E. and A. Limond was much appreciated. A solo and chorus " Sailor's Song," by Mr. A. Grissoldand Company came next, after vhich Messrs George Horsley and George Ball gave a scene from "King John." The "Penalty strollers' made their first appearance and gave a capital rendering of a glee "Com- rades' song,of Hope." A humorous and original ref,ita- tion by Mr. J. Isherwood " Murphy's cure fora cowld " caused plenty of laughter. A song "The waters of the Deep "by Mr. W. Limond was given in fine style' as was also a solo and chorus "Scarlet and Blue" by Mr. E. Parsons and company. This completed the first part, of the programme and during the interval short ad- dresses were given by the Rev. S. D. Thomas and Mr. S. Harvey. The principal item in the second part was "The Middleton District Council's Discussion on the lEducation Bill." several of the bachelors representing the members of the Council and delegates from the Dis- trict School Board. The individual parts were well taken and the audience showed their appreciation by loudly applauding. The concert closed with the singing of the National Anthem. Mr. W. Teesdale presided at the piano in a very able manner. The proceeds are to bedevoted to the above funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Literary and Philosophical Society. —Election</head>
        <p>Literary and Philosophical Society. —Election</p>
        <p>of Officers.</p>
        <p>Monday nights' meeting of the Literary and Philosophical Society was the annual meeting of the Session, and the election of officers took place. Mr. J. D. Buckton, the retiring president presided. Mr. William Long proposed that Mr. F. G. Riuldock be president for the ensuing year, and in doing so alluded to the effie eat services which had been rendered. by Mr. Buckton. He was one of those men who was supposed to be idle and have nothing to do, and per- haps, on that account he was the busiest amongst them (Hear, hear, and laughter.) He proposed a hearty vote of thanks to him for his services. (Applause)..— Mr. J. It. Young seconded, and said his acquaintance with Mr. RwMock was of a more intimate character than Mr. Long's, and he could assure the meeting that Mr. Ruadoek was in every respect able to till the office of president. He had known him since he came to Warrington, and was perfectly persuaded from the personal knowledge of him and the lectures he had delivered that he would make a successful president. (Hear, hear.) The motion was carried unanimOusly.- -Mr. Thomas May moved that Mr. &amp;Atoll he appoint ed vice-president, Mr. W. Silcock (hon. treasurer); Mr. G. Carruthers (hon. secretary), and the Rev. M. Stevenson, Major Appleton, and Messrs. A. J. Jolley, C. S. Burgess, F. W. Monks, and A. Bennett, the Council.—Mr. Atkin seconded, and the motion was carried.—Mr. Silcock made a short statement, as to the financial position of the society, from which it appeared that there was a balance in hand of :ea— On the-motion of Mr. Buckton, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Hon. Treasurer and the Hon. Secretary for their services during the past 12 months, —A paper was afterwards read by Mr. C. B. Roy- lance Kent, M.A.., barrhiter-at-law, on " The Life and Work of Joseph Priestley," a report of which will be found elsewhere.</p>
        <p>Vir4,IITTED a Knod Git5TR ft 4.14 SRVA. gond wages. Apply Mrs. BOTTOMLEY, 9, Milton place, Asht under-Lyne.</p>
