      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>IMPORTANT TO ALL.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT TO ALL.</p>
        <p>Every person's future is, to a certain extent, in his own hand. Many a future which might have been brilli- ant has been darkened by lack of the spirit of self-help. 3lany a home, which might have been gladdened by life and health, and comfort, has been saddened by poverty, illness, and death, in many eases wholly preventible. The spring and summer months are, from a sanitary point of view, among the most important of the year. Due care may now mean a healthy and happy summer and autumn, and robust constitution to meet the trials of next winter. Neglect may now mean a joyless summer, a trying autumn, and a broken constitution to succumb under the attacks of any exceptional trial. There are, indeed, few that do not feel the system needs recruiting after the trying winter they have passed through. With some it is" only a little cough," with others, " a tendency to bronchitis," with others "not quite up to the mark," "a sort of all-over sinking feeling," " to.weak to do anything about the house," "these recurring headaches," " no appetite for my food," etc. These and dozen others are common expressions, which all point to the urgent need otsorne good tonic. What change of air can do for you in a few months time—if you are no worse than you are now—a course of some good tonic mixture, a reliable medicine of established reputation and of proved virtue will do now. There are several tonic mixtures to be had, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegitable Tonic. It has never been known to ; send for a pamphlet of testimonials, and read the voluntary testimony therein contained. Then get Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, which will prove to be your best self-help. We have no sympathy with those geople who always com- plain of being ill and miserable and out of sorts, when a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters would soon, set them right. Sold in bottles, 2s. 9d.and 4s. 6d. each by all chemists and patent medicine vendors, or direct from the proprietors: The Quinine Bitters Manufactur ing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales, carriage free by parcel post. Beware of imitations. See the name of "Gwilym Evans" on label, sten-Ip, and bottle; and refuse any preparation offered as a substitute for</p>
        <p>in the presence of a large crowd.—They were bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs. OBSTRUCTION.—On the evidence of P.C. Baxendale, Joseph Yarwood, 52 West-street, was fined in the costs, 3s. Gd., for allowing a horse and cart to stand in Horse- market-street for twenty minutes on February 27th. DISORDERLY YOUTHS.—WiIIiaM Walton, 8 Hood- ; John Bate, 111 Hesketh-street ; Thomas Riley, 134 Evelyn-street; and Patrick Bennett, 233 Liverpool- road, were summoned for making loud and unseemly noises in Liverpool-road at eleven o'clock on the night of March lst.—Police Constable Andrews said the boys were shouting and singing, and the last two (who pleaded not guilty) refused to give their names, and had to be brought to the Police station. A gentleman complained to him about the conduct of the defendants.— D efendants were each fined ss. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—For making use of obscene language in Lower Ship Yard, Thomas Dockerty, Mary Dockerty, and Winifred Mason, all of Ship Yard, were fined ss. and costs each.—P.C. Cooper (20) proved the Case.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Bafore Aldermen Sutton. (in the chair),</p>
        <p>and Shaw Green, and Dr. Fox. NEGLIGENT FATHERS SENT TO PRISON.—John Mitchell bricklayers' labourer, 20, Ship-yard, was charged with neglecting to maintain his family, in con- sequence of which one child had become chargeable to the common fund of the Warrington Union.—Mr. B. Shearer, who prosecuted on behalf of the Board of Guardians stated that one of the prisoner's children had been Chargeable since September last. He had had several opportunities given to him to take the child away, but he had not taken advantage of them. He had not paid a penny for many months towards the sup- port of his children, and he was one of the class of men who were helping to keep the Workhouse full of children.—Prisoner stated that owing to dizziness he had not been able to work.—Mr. Shearer said the man had not been properly 'sober or 12 months.—The prisoner was committed to gaol for a month with hard labour.- -Thomas Clynes, labourer, Rose and Crown-street, was charged with a like offence, in respect to his wife and five children. He admitted a previous conviction for a similar offence.—Mr. Shearer said the prisoner's four children were admitted to the Workhouse on the 24th August last. The prisoner was apprehended and sent to goal for a month with hard labour. He had since done nothing to maintain his family. Prisoner was a boat discharger at Messrs. Crosfield's, and earned good wages. He had given his wife no assistance for a considerable time. The family had cost the Guardians close upon £3o.—Evidence having been given by the wife, Alder- man Sutton said the prisoner would be committed to gaol for three:months,with hard labour,and he thoroughly , deserved the punishment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>GWILYM EVANS' QUININE, 13ITTERS.</head>
        <p>GWILYM EVANS' QUININE, 13ITTERS.</p>
        <p>BEFORE TIE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>IMPUDENT THEFT BY A SOLDIER. At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday, before Mr. Birchall and other magistrates, David McDonald, who said he was 19 years of age, was charged with steal- ing a leather purse containing 4s. I.ld. from the shop of William Marsh, provision dealer, Mersey-street at 11.50 the previous morning and also with being a deserter from the 6th Enniskillen Dragoonsstationedat Curragh Camp, Treland.—Superintendent Monaghan stated that at 11.50 on Friday morning Mrs. Marsh placed the purse under a butter slab behind the counter and went into the kitchen. A short time previously she had seen the prisoner and another man looking through the shop window. Onhearing a. noise she looked in the shop and saw the prisoner behind the counter with the purse inhis ha.nd. On seeing her,prispner rushed out of the shop but Mrs. Marsh followed him. Informa- tion was given to the police and he was arrested and the money was found in his possession. He had thrown the purse into an outbuilding at the rear of Blackhurst- street. He (Superintendent Monaghan) interviewedthe prisoner at the Police Station and after some question- ing he admitted being a deserter from the Enniskillen Dragoons. Prisoner was a professional thief, and had been convicted seven times.—The magistrates sentenced prisoner to two months' imprisonment with hard labour at the expiration of which term he will be handed over to a military escort.—Superintendent Monaghan was recommended for the usual reward for the apprehension of a deserter.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL WIFE ASSAULT AT WARRINGTON: A sentence of three months' hard labour was inflicted upon Michael Murphy, 11, James-street, Warrington, at the Borough Police Court, on Monday, for committing an aggravated assault on his wife Catherine, en Saturday. Superintendent Monaghan stated that the prisoner went home at about thne o'clock on Saturday afternoon, under the influence of drink, and sent for some beer. He and his wife commenced to quarrel about the chil- dren's clothing, and he was about to strike the com- plainant when she went into the back yard. He threw a jug at her, which smashed on the window sill, and he then threw an ornament at her. He afterwards followed her into the yard, knocked her down, and struck and kicked her. He got a bottle and struck her several times on thehead with it,inflicting a wound three inches long. She fainted, and lost a great deal of blood, .and was afterwards taken to the Infirmary by her children. Her husband was arrested in the evening, by Sergeant Barnett.—Evidence bearing out this statement having been given by prisoner's wife and son, prisoner stated that his wife who was drunk, threw a cup at him first. He was so aggravated that he committed the assault.— Sergeant Barnett said the woman was perfectly sober at 9,15. When arrested prisoner made no complaint as to his wife assaulting him.—Prisoner, who made his sixth appearance, was sentenced to three months im- prisonment with hard labour. A MOTHER'S DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday some shocking details were given of the disgraceful conduct of a mother with reference to her children. Mr. Hayward was the presiding magistrate. The de- fendant was named Annie Farrington, living at 27, Bos- tock-street, who appeared in the dock with an infant in her arms. She was charged with neglecting her four children, one of whom was the infant, in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. She admitted that the children were badly clothed.—Mr. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., said Inspector Hackett had had the woman under observation for three years, and had warned her from time to time. There had been improvements and retrogressions, but at last the Inspector had been com- pelled to take legal proceedings against her because she would not keep the children clean and they were a danger to themselves and all the other children at school. P.C. Woods had describedthe house as unfit for habitation, owing to its filthy condition. When she saw the Inspector coming to pay her a sur- prise visit she rushed to the door, but in her haste overturned a table at which two women were sitting drinking beer. One stout old lady was upset and fell amongst some ashes near the fire grate, and was sur- rounded by beer which had been spilt all over the flocrr. All the cups and bottles on the table were smashed. Of I course that incident had nothing to do with the present case except to show the cause of neglect ; there was food always to be found in the house. Inspector Hackett gave evidence and stated that at one time he allowed the case to drop owing to improvement in defendant's conduct, but at last he had been compelled to summon her. The mistress of the school the children attended complained that they were not fit to mix with the other children.—Alderman Smethurst Did your husband know that this case wag coming on this morn- ing ?—Defendant replied in the affirmative and stated that he might possibly be in Court. His name was called, but there was no response.—The Clerk: Will you sign the pledge if the magistrates adjourn the case, and give you another chance ?—Defendant promised to do so.—lnspector Hackett said the prisoner had gone to live in another house.—The case was then adjourned for a month, and the Chairman said the defendant's conduct seemed to savour of laziness.—lnspector Hackett: Laziness and drunkenness.—Ellen Johnson, of 16, Bostock-street, had preferred a charge against the defendant of using threats toward her,in consequence of the defendant thinking that she had informed the Inspector, but in the circumstances she consented to allow the case to stand over for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Minor Cases.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. Hayward (in the chair). Alder- man Smethurst, Messrs. Thornton and Wright. DRUNK IN CHARGE AND NO LIGHT.—For having been drunk while in charge of a horse and trap in KgPilesford- road, on March 4th, and for not having a lighted lamp attached to the vehicle, Arthur Wade, farther, Swineyard Hall farm, High Legh,was fined for the first offence 10s. th and costs and for e second ss. including costs.—P.C. Hardy said the defendant drove along Ktmtsford-road at a furious rate. SMART FINE FOR GAMBLING.—Peter Richardson, 1, Philip-street, and Elijah Rathbone, 45, Lythgoes-lane, youths, were summoned for playing pitch and toss in houghton-street on the Ist March.—P.C. Clough proved the case, and said the defendants were :vvith three others. The lads had a club and payed one another's fines.— They were fined 10s. and costs each. FIGHTING.—Thomas Towley, 63 Foundry-street, and Patrick McDonald, Rose-and-Crown-street, were summoned for committing a breach of the peace by fighting in ft idge-street on February 286.—P.C. Tolly said the me, were fighting at the end of Rylands-street</p>
        <p>" Hith,rto certAn Dutch Cocoas have been oon- sldered the h'ghest in quality of any in the market, but</p>
