      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Messrs. W. Timmins (in the chair) J. Ockleston and S. Taylor. TEMPORARY AUTHOR ivy.—William Kendrick was granted permission to sell at " Wilsons Hotel "lately occupied by William Wildgoose. SCHOOL BOARD CASES :—The following persons were fined, exclusive of costs, for neglecting to send their children to school ;—William Gibbons, Owen Garvie, Paul Jones, 5s ; William Belfield, 7s. 6d. ; William Taylor (two cases) 15s. in each case; Albert Ravens- croft, 10s. Messrs. Bowyer and Howard, school attend- ance officer,proved the cases. A PAUPER'S FUNERAL.—Mr. W. Sutton (assistant overseer) was granted an order on the County Council for certain expenses incurred in the funeral: of a dead body found in the Bridgewater Canal. POOR FATE.—There was a number of poor rate orders Mr. Sutton, assistant overseer, proving the cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>NEARLY A BREAKDOWN.</head>
        <p>NEARLY A BREAKDOWN.</p>
        <p>One of the direct results of kidney disease is a de- ranged nervous system. It may show itself in many ways—violent headaches, neuralgia, numbness, unsteady hands, impaired speech. But the cause is the kidneys. They are unwell, they are leaving poison in the blood, and the poisoned blood is starving the nerves. Mr. D. Latter, of Stradbroke, Eye, Suffolk, tells an interesting story of kidney trouble. He suffered greatly from neuralgia. pains in the back and loins, weakness, loss of appetite, numbness in the fingers, cold hands and feet—all symptoms of poor blood and defective circulation. Mr. Larter found no cure until he tried Dodd's Kidney -Pills. They cured his kidneys. The kidneys began to do their work, to cleanse the blood and send it, rich and nourishing to the,body. To-day he is en- tir?ly cured, and "feels a new man." Simply because he cured his kidneys and went to the root of his trouble. Every sufferer should do the same; the sure remedy for disease is Dodd's Kidney Pills. Price two and nine from Chemists, or the Dodds Medicine Co., 23, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>£45 FOR AN INJURED HAND. Boy Recovers Damages friyn Messrs.</head>
        <p>£45 FOR AN INJURED HAND. Boy Recovers Damages friyn Messrs.</p>
        <p>Crosfield and Sans.</p>
        <p>At the]Warrington County Court on Thursday last week j before His Honour judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., a boy j named HaroldlLeigh,suing by his father, Elliott Leigh,of Sandy-lane, Lymm, under the Employers' Liability Act, claimed ,C7B from Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, for injuries sustained to his left hand and arm by being crushed between the cogwheels and the re- volving bar of a machine known as the " hachior."—Mr. Smith (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) represented the plaintiff, and Mr. Byrne (instructed by Mr. F. R. Roberts) appeared for the defendant company. Mr. Smith said there was no question at all as to liability to be fought out. The sole question between his learned friend and himself was one of how much damage should be paid. The accident took place on the 30th of August at the defendants works. The boy who was 15 years of age was employed at a machine called the "hachior" connected with which were certain cogwheels and a revolving bar. These parts of the machine, they contended, were not sufficiently fenced or guarded, and since the accident had been so guarded. Further than that, they contended that a man in the employ of the defendants named Arthur Berry, ordered the plaintiff to clean that machine whilst in ' motion. That of course, was a serious offence under the Factory Acts. The boy sustained serious injuries to his hand and would not be able to properly close his ' hand again. He was cleaning the machine when his hand was drawn into the cogwheels, and his fingers, thumb and the fleshy part of his hand were crushed. He called upon a boy to step the machine and it was stopped. After he got his hand out he went to the Infirmary where his hand was stitched up. The boy had suffered great pain, and two months after the accident Dr. Ferguson found the hand still tender and not fit to be worked.</p>
        <p>His Honour: How do you arrive at the sum whichi you nominally claim?</p>
        <p>, Mr. Smith iv At the time of the accident the boy was earning 10s. a week and we claim 156 weeks or three years wages. Mr. Byrne said the boy's average earnings during the past three years were Bs. sd. per week. - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith said it was true- that the boy started at a lower wage, but under the Employers Liability Act, His Honour could take three years earnings at the present rate.</p>
        <p>• Harold Leigh was then called and bore out Mr. Smith's statement as to how the accident happened.—Cross- examined by Mr. Byrne, witness admitted that Dr. Curtis told him in November last that he was fit to go back to work. He did not go back.—Re-examined by Mr. Smith: The firm had offered to pay £2 6s. com- pensation and afterwards £lO 10s. I Dr. Ferguson said that when he examined the boy the I hand was very tender. He would never be able to use the hand as before the accident, or to close it entirely. I For the defence, Dr. James D. Liston, surgeon to the sick club in connection with Messrs.Crosfleld's works,was called. He said he was not in any way under the con- trol of the firm. The boy came to see him a week after he sustained the injury and he gave him a certificate to obtain sick pay. He did not take any part in the treat- ment of the injury until the boy was discharged from the infirmary. The wound was then completely healed. He saw the boy weekly until October 22nd when witness formed the opinion that he was able to resume his work., Witness was of the opinion that any ordinary employment would be a good thing for the hand. Dr. Curtis stated that he examined the boy on behalf of the defendant company on December Ist. The scar was quite healed and witness was of opinion that suit- able work would be the best treatment. He advised the boy to return to work and also advised the firm that he was fit to return to suitable employment, by which he meant carrying packing or similar occupations. Mr. Byrne addressed his Honour and at the outset admitted that the company were at fault in regard to not having the machine fenced and allowing the boy to clean it whilst in motion. • Mr. Byrne then went in detail into the question of amount,, and said he desired to be quite fair in the matter. He asked has Honour to allow the boy for loss of wages between August 30th and February 23rd-21 weeks at 9s. per week. He called attelitiofi to the testi- mony of Drs. Liston and Curtis as to the boy's ability to work on February 23rd. Dr. Liston had said that the boy was fit to work on October 22nd, but he (Mr. Byrne) thought that was too soon, and would notask his Honour to draw a hard and fast line according to Dr. Liston's opinion. He contended that there was no per- manent injury to the hand. Mr. Cuthbert Smith replied, and said he ventured to think that the boy's hand would never be as perfect as it formerly was. His Honour came to the conclusion that any violation of the Factory Acts which had been made by the firm was entirely unconscious on their part. He regretted that the offer of £2 16s. had been made,as it was entirely inadequate and impressed the case unfavourably. He awarded the plaintiff £43 damages with coats on Scale C. -</p>
      </div>
