      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, March 13, 1903 FASHIONS OF TO-DAY. MI EMINENT SURGEON'S °MUM. I</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 13, 1903 FASHIONS OF TO-DAY. MI EMINENT SURGEON'S °MUM. I</p>
        <p>The opinion of the late Sir Andrew Clarke on con- stipation is worth consideration by all sufferers from this ailment. He said that not only was constipation bad in itself,but that it was the chief cause of &amp;Impala in girls and women. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans are a 'mild vegetable cure for constipation, suitable for the weakest constitutions. Many doctors now prescribe Bile Beans, and so great is their use as a family medicine that over three-quarters of a million doses are now taken every day. They act directly on the liver and stomach, not like mere purgatives which act on the bowels and weaken them. The bile is the natural purgative of the body, and by regnlating its secretion Bile Beans end constipation in the surest and most certain manner.</p>
        <p>The new materials are all suited for draping and lend themselves readily to the graceful folds which faQhion demands. There is nothing which equals, or is likely to supplant, voile in this respect, as for an afternoon or evening toilette it is still unrivalled. It is a fabric which is at once light and durable and etands a wonderful amount of hard wear. Of course there are many novelties among this season's materials which have much to recommend them, and whose attractions are so manifest as tc ensure a continued success. Among the most admirable may be mentioned the new open. meshed canvases adorned at intervals With a light floral pattern, wrought in silks, or woven in a truly wonderful fashion to represent different styles of lace. In the latter instance the colour is usually a deep cream, or twine colour, and it is made up over chine silk of a slightly deeper tone. Another material which follows up its successes of previous seasons is alpaca, and the latest are of an exceedingly silky and supple • description, very different from The original style, which wa.A hard and stiff, but which wore much longer than is the ease with</p>
        <p>-E'''.,,, ,rtiii., 4:•..:.:A.,;:::,,:,-Y Iftl,i'?illillik''' ..)1;.:‘',‘,'.•..,n.."', 4 , 1 I ~</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>COLONIAL QUESTION.</head>
        <p>A COLONIAL QUESTION.</p>
        <p>(By Mr. H. WHITHAM.)</p>
        <p>These are stirring times and one needs to be pretty wide awake to be at all abreast with them.</p>
        <p>- Since January 2nd I have been unable to guide a ! pen, having crushed off a goodly portion of the middle finger of my right hand. However not being able to work in any way did not mean inability to hold a book and read, so it was not all loss. Eve, book reading has to make way for the newspaper, an-c, when one has gone through the morning issues, and had a whiff of fresh air, there are the half-dozen evening editions waiting for perusal. It would be an easy matter to allow these to go by, but what can one do when the news boys, are yelling out at the topf of the voices the disturbing . news of a declaration o war? The paper is hastily opened and you sea at once in great flaring red head- lines that "war is declared." England, Germany, and Italy, are allied against America, France and Russia. Looking further into the matter it does appear that there is a possibility of this very Istrange jumbling together of the foremost nations of the earth may result in what may prove to be a worlds I cataclysm. Fortunately this deluge has been averted and once more England's trouble with Venezuela has been settled by the aid of diplomacy. Before leaving this question I must say I have yet to meet an EngliSh- man, or an American for that matter, who can under- stand why the British Government should join hands with Germany. It is well-known that the latter nation has and keeps one great object before it, and that is to colonize some portion of the American continent in spite of all the MonrBe doctrinc.s that may exist. This doctrine is clear enough, and should be reasonable enough too, to Englishmen, since it had its inception in the brain of an English statesman,George Canning. Canada was a British Colony before the birth of doctrine, and it may be taken as an unwritten agreement that Canada either as a colony of Great Britain or as an Independent State can only exist and pursue her peaceful and developing course by England and America joining hands in defence of the Monroe doetrine. It would be well if Britishers gave a little of their time to the study of the Canadian question and a little farther on 1 will attempt to show why. In the meantime let us stray a little. The tendency, as I take it, of the growth of nations is, like most other of the burning questions of the day' towards absorption. The two great empires of the future will be the British Empire and the Russian Empire with the American continent as a sort of wedge between. We scarcely realize what the Russian Empire is or means. Take Siberia, for instance, in its immensity. It stretches through 130 degrees of longitude and possessing one-ninth of all the land sur- face of the globe it almost staggers one. The United States and all its possessions, and all of Europe except Russia could be put into Siberia, with land 4 . enough left to make thirty-five states like Connecticut, and one of Russia's latest acquisitions--Manchuria— will make seventy more.</p>
        <p>This is a big question, and a little beyond. me, but as the food question is .interesting British statesmen just now, and rightly so, I think the common people should be urged to study it also. Out of the'Veneznelan question the Alaskan ques- tion had a re-birth. ' The settlement of this strictly on the lines of the agreement between the Powers would give Canada one of her most useful ports for develop- ing hef grain lauds. The most foolish arrangement towards this settlement has been made in the appoint- ment of the commission which meets in London at an early date. Three Americans meet three Britishers, and this can only mean a deadlock, as the whole six will be sufficiently patriotic to stand by the views of the nations they represent, and so this question which is of superlative importance to Canada will go on until its smouldering embers burst into a flame. In the meantime America will build and control the Isthmian Canal, and the proposed railway through Alaska will materialize, and then what of Canada?</p>
        <p>Now let us look a little closer at Canada. To begin I with the Dominion, like the Commonwealth of Australia, and the rest of Britain's self-governing colonies, has exclusive control of its own taxation and expenditure. It has the army and navy of Great Britain to defend it,!and towards the maintenance of which it contributes an insignificant sum. The in- habitants of Canada have all the liberties and but few of the burdens of the Britishers. They are not very anxious to take a part in the Federation idea, and are not thinking of sending a representative to West- minster. As a matter of fact Canada seems to be just a little inclined to think that the British Government may throw her over in her effort to court favour with the States. Surely British diplomacy, is capable of something better than this,—surely ! There is yet another truth concerning Canada, and that is that her invasion is being surely and silently effected. Who are the patriots in Canada? We may accept the des- cendants of the Loyalist Refugees who settled in British North America after the Revolutionary war. These with the English and Scotch emigrants and their descendents make up the trustworthy Canadian patriots There are fewer British and Irish-born people in Canada to-day by nearly 100,000 than there were thirty Iyears ago. The number of emgrants from countries lother than those embraced in the British Empire was 1278.804 in 1901, as against only 131,083 in 1881. Of the 49,149 immigrants that entered Canada in 1901 only 11,810 were of British and Irish birth. The English speaking people are being crowded out of Quebec. In this province the French speaking people are most numer- ous, and double in numbers every twenty-five years. They have no love for the British Government. Their customs, language and laws are French. They are more French than the French themselves and yet have no love for the French Government. They are in- tensely Roman Catholics and resent recent French legislation. And yet all this does not explain the true and real inwardness of the Canadian invasion. The invasion is industrial, and being American will be fully appreciated by all thinking Englishmen. I may say just here that the America of to-day is a very different country to the America of fifteen or even ten years ago. Now merchants and manufacturers are all on the alert seeking out fresh woods and pastures in which to give full play to their energy. The home market has failed entirely to consume the products of the mines. ft has failed completely to find paying investments for the rapidly increasing capital that is rolling in so abundantly to the capitalist. True, there is a large residuum class steeped in poverty as there will be until the labour question begins to receive a more thought- ful study. Business is, and has been for sometime going forward by leaps and bounds, and yet strikes are of every day occurence. The miners strike has come to an end, and the Commission called together lby the President of the United States has been the Imeans of eliciting information that has proved beyond a doubt that the miners condition was simply deplor- able. In this city of San Francisco a strike of the bricklayers has been on for two months. The men ask for 245. a day, and the employers offer 225., and still the men stand firm. In all trades and occupa- tions there is a fearful unrest, no one being capable of I saying where it will end. Some go so far as to ! prophesy that the labour war will end in civil war land yet there is a strong optimistic feeling that the ; good sense of the nation at large. will be able to solv3 i the problem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</head>
        <p>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</p>
        <p>TAKE VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. This marvellously successful remedy surpasses anything in the healing art and has the largest sale in the world.</p>
        <p>The remarkable rapidity with which Veno'i Lightning Cough Cure absolutely cures the worse cases of COUGHS, COLDS, BRONCHITIS, ASTHMA, INFLUENZA, CATARRH, and CHILDREN'S COUGHS has gained for it scientific recognition throughout the world. GRANVILLE H. SHARPE, F.C.S.' Analyst, 11 and 12, Gt. Tower Street, London, says in his certificate of analysis :—" lam of opinion that Veno's Lightning Cough Cure will prove a valuable remedy in the cure of coughs, colds, asthma, and all bronchial complaints." GEO. KNOX, M.D., 256, Second Avenue, Pittsburg, writes :—" Veno's Lightning Cough Cure excels in chronic coughs and bronchitis, and is especially good for children." Ask for Veno's LIGHTNING COUGH CURE at Chemists,price Is. lid. and 2s. 9d., or post free from the Veno Drug Co., Hulme, Manchester.</p>
