      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>PRETTY DRESS.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY DRESS.</p>
        <p>the soft variety. Some of the prettiest are flowered chine,e, while spots and stripes are still considered modish. Soit silks inserted with lace will also be much worn, but it is still rather early to consider their possibilities seriously, except for evening gowns. A pretty evening dress for a girl of fifteen or sixteen, given in the first sketch, is made of soft silk of an ivory shade of white. The skirt is accordeon pleated in the latest style, the pleats narrowing off into nothing at the waist. The sleeves are loose and are made likewise of accordeon pleated silk, caught in at the wrists by bands of lace insertion, matching that which. trims the bodice and cape-like collar, of flow- ered .net. The lace insertion and net are of a faint twine colour, and the satin ribbon which surrounds the waist and from there falls in two ends down the side, is of the same tone of colour as the lace. The costume is simple and girlish, and yet very smart and up-to-date in style. A dress like this would be found very useful in summer, as it could then be worn out of doors on gala occasions. Indeed there is much in this little gown which could be copied by a girl who has quite emerged from the schoolroom and is fairly launched into society. I should suggest, however, in that case that the waist band is broad, after the style of a Swiss belt, fastening in front with a brilliant buckle.</p>
        <p>t( .. ...e 1 .... ' i.f..:4l\N, 7t isi 111 1 /0, ' 4 4 /,,, vo, , . , ~,,,k %c:•.)",,,,c1„, ,\e", s ' ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FLORAL TOQUE.</head>
        <p>A FLORAL TOQUE.</p>
        <p>Liberty satins are also very much worn in the evening, the skirt being cut quite plain but very full and caught in..° the waist band by small pleats. Hats are very charming., and nothing looks so modish for dressy occasions than a toque of violets such as is depicted, trimmed solely by a handsome osprey, caught by a jewelled brooch. The crown is ilat ond is also composed of these small flowers. Floral toques will be much worn early in the season, while hats and toques of roe-red tulle or illusion bid faia to be also greatly in vogue. Some of the smartest hats have motifs until appliqu6s of fine silky straw which simulates lace work in a wonderful way. This style of trimming is especially suited to tulle toques as it serves to strengthen this very perishable foninla :ion and contrasts pleasantly in its yellowish tint with the dull white of the tulle. Variety is to be found on all sides in the millinery world, and hats and toques too, become every style of face and colouring. There are French hats, which turn up at the back and come well over the face in front, while others again have wide and high. brims, turned right back from the face, with a big bow or bunch of flowers, or a bird of magpie plumage between the hair and the brim, somewhat after the Incroyable 'style.</p>
        <p>I{ 0' igt t% ,i, 4 ) ~,,, 0 t4r.i,,....g t 7; .giti,s,' IA - ,4,1,41, ,4-,-,,, i '1 4vo tv" L 0 ,ip,, 1 , ) rik i f 4,4 i ii, --, ih,.. / I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>USEFUL CA r.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL CA r.</p>
        <p>A very useful and popular style of coat is shown in the last sketch, made with a triple cape. The coat is loose and roomy, and therefore easily slipped on over any kind of costume. It is lined with a light tartan silk, which also is used for the deep cuffs of the wide sleeves. For travelling, whether by sea or by land, a coat of this kind will be found of immense comfort, as early in the year the air has a trick of becoming cold and chilly when the sun has sunk below the horizon, which makes one welcome a cosy wrap with enthusiasm. Some of these coats are three-quarters length, while others again come quite to the edge of the skirt. The materials employed in their construction range from a dark navy blue or green cloth to pale putty colour, which verges narrowly on white. For race meetings the pale colours are most suitable, with a touch of lace in the collar, while instead of the tiiple capes of cloth a wide collar of strong point lace should be substituted. The same style of coat will also be fashionable made in taffetas, which of late has undergone quite a transformation and is now much softer and wears longer than before. The seams are, in some instances, strapped with fine cloth of the same shade, while fancy patterned ribbons are used far the same purpose.</p>
        <p>COQUETTE,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>-- 1 The King has frequently given evidence of his friend- ship for the American nation, and further proof of this is afforded by his Majesty's promise to allow Queen Victoria's Jubilee presents to be sent to the St Louis exhibition next year. This is intended to exceed in magnitude and importance, all previous exhibitions, not excepting those of Chicago and Paris and the Jubilee presents will be accorded the place of honour in the Palace of Art. , 'They cannot fail to make a very interesting exhibit, and the King's example should have the effect of in- ducing the represe4tatives of British arts and industries to take a larger interest in the St Louis exhibition. It is with this object that Miss Florence Hayward, dm special commissioner of the Exhibition Committee, is now on a visit to this country. It says something for feminine achievements that the representative of the greatest of International Exhibitions should be a woman, who is visiting Europe not from any special feminine standpoint, but as an authority on the great industrial progress of the world. The Marchioness of Londonderry,like Lady Lansdowne is a great hostess, and intends to entertain a great deal during the London season. Her dinner parties, and semi-political receptions, at Londonderry House, are always attended by the leading people in Society, and the King and Queen have more than once honoured the Marchioness with their presence. So also has the Ger- man Emperor, who, when he first visited London, was entertained to dinner by Lady Londonderry, and also by Lady Dudley, but of late years Dudley House has been !, occupied by Mr. J. B. Robinson, the South African millionaire.</p>
        <p>The ever-increasing number of Society scandals that have been made public, are being freely supplemented by others, which, though not appearing in the Law Courts, are no less openly discussed in the clubs, and other places. Mostly they refer to gambling practices, and a good deal of sympathy is felt for a young debutante who has been taken to houses where she has sustained embarrassing losses at cards. Another story relates to a titled personage and hiswife who are openly accused of cheating, the wife, by a system of signs and telegraphy, enabling the husband to win large sums of money.</p>
        <p>There have been similar scandals before in Society, but it is added that the couple were not at all abashed "when found out," and regarded such practices as not ; unusual. Only recently a titled member of anhonoured ' family published a book on his experiences in penal servitude, and it would seem that he is not alone among those of aristocratic birth who apparently see nothing in fraud and swindling, to be ashamed of. There does not appear to be any foundation for the rumour that twopenny-pieces are to be issued, although as a matter of fact silver coins of that value are pro- duced at the mint as part of the issue of Maundy money. The introduction of twopenny-pieces would certainly be unwelcome to the ministers and officers of churches, who have reason to complain that a great many people who could afford more put threepenny-pieces in the plate. The Rev. C. H. Spurgeon is said to have referred somewhat scathingly to this practice, remarking that many people sang the hymn Were the whole realm of Nature mine, It were an offering far too small; and at the same time feeling in their pockets for a threepenny-bit, rubbing the edges to make sure that it was not a fourpermy-piece. Home manufactured brocades are being extensively used for court gowns, a fact which must be particularly pleasing to the Princess of Wales. Her Royal Highness following the custom of her mother, the late Duchess of Teck, wears none save British and Irish silks, satins, and brocades, the latter fabric, and Irish poplin being first favourites with the Princess. Very few trans- parent trains will be seen at the forthcoming Courts. Over a carpet, they are fairly successful, but it has been found that on the polished floor of the apartment, in which the Courts take place, they invariably collapse into a wisp-like arrangement, and that this is anything but graceful, or conducive to the dignity of the wearer. In the embroidery world, the huge bunches of grapes with their attendant vine leaves, and tendrils, have now to be numbered among ideas of the past, but more graoeful designs representing—for example—lahumum, lilac,,acticia, with long trailing branches of foliage, will quite reconcile us to this loss. The flowers are worked out in soft silk chiffon, the stems in chenille, and the leaves in soft ribbon, diamond dewdrops glittering here and there upon them. In and around these designs are fine traceries in gold and silver thread, and here and there appear opal sequins and mother-of-pearl pail- lettes. Very beautiful are these elaborate embroideries, which, together with applique medallions of lace, are playing such an importnnt part in the building of all the smartest ball, and tea-gowns, and evening wraps. !Where is no general wearing of sombre hues this Lent. Pale soft pastel costumes p-re still in greatest favour,- it . .</p>
        <p>is true,but the accessory colours are aniong the brightest and most startling possible. Vivid orange, brilliant sapphire, emerald green. bright pink, are great favourite touches of one or the other of these colours, appearing in the hat or toque, at the neck and waistbelt, and in the underskirt flounce. In the selection of mange, considerable care should be exercised. To the very few is this colour really becoming, especially in the more vivid tints, and in any case it should be kept well away from the face. Irish industries are just now attracting a good deal of attention, as in addition to the sale at Londonderry House, on St. Patrick's Day, there has been this week an exhibition of Irish home made carpets at the Grafton Gallery in London. This industry was started in the West of Ireland some time ago, to benefit a very poor district in Donegal and Galway, and it has this advant- age that good hand-made carpets will always realise their worth in money and are not so dependent upon fashion as are lace, tweeds, and similar cottage indus- tries. The carpets are hand-tufted, the same as Turkish or Persian carpets, and are in mosaics of small woollen squares, in artistic designs. Judging by the interest taken in the Exhibition there is likely to be a good demand for these carpets,and the industryshould enable many a poor Irish cottar to earn a better livelihood. There is much to be said for the Bishop of Lincoln's plea for the better observance of Sunday. On this sub- vet we seem to have gone from one extreme to another. In former years there was perhaps too much disposition to spend Sunday in gloom and depression, but now it is associated with dinners, luncheons, bridge parties, excursions, and entertainments, of all kinds, and this change is equally marked amongst all classes of society, from the most fashionable to the humblest. Sunday work is on the increase, at one end of the scale, and Sunday entertainments at the other. Both are demoralis- ing physically and intellectually, and it is not surprising that Dr. Ring should protest against the disappearance of Sunday as "a day of rest."</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SHIP CANAL ACCIDENT. Narrow Escape of a Runcorn Man.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL ACCIDENT. Narrow Escape of a Runcorn Man.</p>
        <p>A Runcorn waterman, named George Hooper, of Bel- vedere, met with a serious accident on the Manchester Ship Canal which might have been attended with fatal results. While working on, a dredger he was violently struck.onthe forehead just over the right eye with a winch handle which had unexpectedly recoiled.. He was rendered unconscious by the blow, and, for three weeks lay in a critical condition. Mr:Hooper did not recover from the effects of the accident—which proved very serious--until he took Dr. Williams pink pills for pale people. Before the acci- dent Mr. Hooper was a particularly clear-headed man and extremely active, but the accident apparently set up some terribly mental disease; for, at intervals after the mishap he suffered from terrible severe pains in his head. Interviewed by a "Warrington Examiner" representa- tive Mr. Hooper said: "After the stitches had been taken out and the wound healed I was .not very long before I began to be troubled with a dull, sickening pain right across my forehead. My memory, hitherto retentive, failed me so that I frequently forgot the captain's orders. The men on the job can tell you that if I was ordered to get three articles I would often bring back but one, having clean forgotten the others. Further I often used to see three or four barges coming alongside when there was only one ; and similarly with other things. Before receiving the blow I And never experi- enced anything like that, and, at times, I was greatly alarmed. For seven or eight years I took all kinds of , medicines. I also consulted several doctors. One of .the doctors I saw told me that the incised scar would have to be lifted, reopened as it was pressing on the brain. In June last year I resolved to consult a specialist, but, in the meantime, while working at the Old Quay, I felt so ill that one Saturday I went to a doctor's for some medicine. While 1 was waiting a young woman told me that Dr. Williams, pink pills had done her a great deal of good, and she advised me to give them a trial. I was so disappointed at trying different things that I thought no more of it at the time. Feeling no• better by the following Saturday J resolved to go to the Liverpool Infirmary but found that the building closed before I could get a suitable train. I then remembered the young woman's advice and purchased a box of Dr. Williams pink pills for pale people. I took the first dose on the Saturday night, two after each of my four meals on Sunday, and such was their immediate effect on me that by Monday morning I was a different man altogether. I took, in all, about half the box. lam all right now and feel better than T have ever done since receiving the blow. "You have no objection, I suppose, to these facts being made public?" "Not at all," was the emphatic reply "I do not care who knows. it is absolutely true. If people want to know anything more about the case, or cannot credit the cure they can send their letters here and I will prove my statement. I attribute my recovery solely tc Dr. Williams Pink Pills for Pale People. Half the pills in the second box I gave to my little girl, who suffered from Chronic bronchitis. Before taking the pills she weighed only 29i pounds; in two weeks she put on 21 pounds. The pills were not purgative but on the contrary, strengthening. Before leaving, the interviewer learned that Mr Hooper knew several people who had benefited by Dr. Williams pink pills, and that he had recommended their use to alibis relations and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
