      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>EDUCATION ACT. TEACHERS' REPRESENTATIVE ON THE</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION ACT. TEACHERS' REPRESENTATIVE ON THE</p>
        <p>NEW AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>Councillor JOHN UPSON has been selected to represent the Warrington and District Teachers' Association on the new education authority. The nomination was male at the annual meeting of the Association held in Heathside Boys' School on Saturday morning. In proposing the nomination of Councillor Upson the President (Mr. STANSFIELD, 8...) referred to the action taken by the Association in regard to obtaining repre- sentation on the education committee, and to Councillor Upson's valuable support at the Council meeting when the constitution of the committee was decided upon. Councillor Upson was, as it were, an object lesson to therest of the Council, illustrating the stamp of teacher seeking this representation. He was also able to correct, on the spot, the mistaken notion of the composition of this association which Aid. Monks had formed, and had given expression to. (Applause). He was able to inform Ald. Monks and the Council that this association was composed only of certificated teachers. Every man and every woman in that associa- tion was either a head teacher or fully qualified to take a head teacher's post that day if necessary. The certi- ficated teachers of Warrington were to be congratulated that Mr. Upson was in the Council and thus able to make this matter clear or they might not to-day have had the privilege of electing a repre- sentative. As it was they knew that the Council had almost unanimously given them that power. (Applause). It was now for them to' decide what kind of representative they desired. He (the president) thought their representative should be a man who had not only teache's' interests, but also the welfare of elementary and other kinds of education in Warrington at heart. He must be a man of broad views. At the same time they must remember that he would be the only man on the authority who would know from practical experience, where the shoe would pinch, and at the same time know how to apply the remedy. They wanted a man, therefore, who would act wisely, and who would have the courage to speak out if necessary. Their representative should be a man who would go in for education itself, and set his face against red taps, fads, and unnecessary and useless returns. They wished the head teachers in their schools to be free to teach and organize and supervise, and not to be mere clerks as he was informed they some- times became in some Board schools. Their representative would also remember that certificated teachers possessed a diploma issued by the Board of Education certifying them to be fully qualified practi- tioners. He should, therefore, set his face against all compulsory certificate hunting. (Applause.) If teachers wished to attend science classes or any other classes he (the president) advised them by all means to do so, but there must be no semblance of compulsion. That had been tried elsewhere, and in every case it had ended in failure. He hoped the Warrington Edu- cation Authority would not make the same mistake. He should like to propose from the chair that Councillor Upson be their representative. He had done excellent work in the Council, and he was sure that in him they would have an admirable representative. (Applause.) Mr. A. COREY seconded the nomination, and Mr. J. DEWHURST supported it. The motion was carried unanimously. Councillor UesoN said that he heartily thanked the association for this mark of their confidence, and he would try to come up to the standardas defined by their president in his speech. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Fire at a School.</head>
        <p>Fire at a School.</p>
        <p>A fire which might have caused considerable damage had it not been soon discovered and ex- tinguished, broke out at St. Pauls' Infant School, Kendrick-street, at about 4.30 on Wednesday morn- ing. At that time a man named Welsh was passing the school, when he saw smoke issuing through one of the windows. He at once went to the fire statioi, and gave the alarm to the superintendent, and when the bell was rung the Deputy Superintendent and a number of firemen turned out with promptitude and started for the scene of the fire with the reel cart, adder, and fire escape. After bursting open the outer gate and a school door they entered the building, and discovered that the floor near the stove was in flames. The fire was, however, soon extinguished. not very much damage being done. A cupboard was burnt as well as a number of books and school registers. The fire is believed to have been due to the overheating of the stove.</p>
      </div>
