      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>WARRINGTON_ BANKRUPTCY COURT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON_ BANKRUPTCY COURT</p>
        <p>- - - FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Registrar Ridgway. A FARMER'S BANKRUPTCY.—James Percival, farmer, of Godscroft Farm, Frothham, attended for his examina- tion in bankruptcy by Mr. Johnson (assistant official receiver.) His statement of affairs showed a deficiencY of £759 12s. 7d. The bankrupt alleged as the causes of failure "High rental, losses of live stock, low prices, and household and personal expenditure in excess of profits."--,--The examination_ was closed.</p>
        <p>Re JAMES LEIGH.—This debtor, residing at 1, St. Austin's-lane, and carrying on business as a baker and provision dealer at 7, Friars' Green, and 21, Penked- yard, Friars Gate, appeared for his public examination in bankruptcy. Mr. Willett represented the . debtor whose statement of affairs showed liabilities amounting to £241 17s. 6d. and assets £4O 12s. Id., leaving a deti- ciency of £2Ol ss. sd. The bankrupt alleged as the causes of failure ' Bad trade, ill-health of my wife: want of capital, dens-price of flour, and household and personal expenditure in excess of profit."He statedithil he started business about 13 a o years without capita; and in May, 1902 he purchased a bakehouse at Penkers - yard for £450. He had not kept any cash book or creditor's ledger, nor while in business had he ascer tamed his financial position. lie became amire of hls insolvency about the 17th December last.—The est amination was closed.</p>
        <p>A GROCER'S SECOND FAlLURE.—Frederick WiMani Hallwood, who carried on business as a grocer and pro- vision dealer at 60 Green-street and at 260 Liverpool' road, was publicly examined by Mr. Johnson (assistant official ieceiver). Mr. Steel represented the debtor.-- The statement of affairs showed that the liabilities were expected to• rank at £B9l 18s. sd. and the assets were estimated to produce £234 3s. 7d. leaving a deficiend of £675 14s. 10d. The causes of failure as alleged by the bankrupt were "Bad trade, bad debts, competition, want of capital." He stated that his former failure in businese as a grocer in Buttermarket-street was in 1896. He then executed a deed of assignment and there under paid a dividend amounting to 3s. 7d. in the £ on liabili- ties amounting to about £1,200. He recommenced busi' ness in Green-street, in April 1897 and was then onll indebted to three creditors"for small amounts. Not having kept sufficient books of account, nor while in businesSi ascertained his financial position, he could not file the prescribed deficiency account, but he attributed his ded-' ciency account. to:—Bad debts, £323 15s, sd.i anticipated loss on realisation of assets, £1.1" 12s. 10d.; and household and personal expenses DI excess of profits £217 6s. 7d. With his pre- sent knowledge he believed he had been insolvent for the last four years or thereabouts. He admitted that be had given credit recklessly without the. consent of creditors, but he was of opinion that if he had continue(' in business he would have collected a larger sum tbi that shown in the statement. Business had fallen on- considerably in consequence of three or four new shol3l having been opened near his business premises. He IoP £36 by the purchase of the shop and dwelling house Green-street. He paid £475 for it, law costs were .g&gt; alterations £l5 and paving and sewering £43, making total of £561. The sum realized at the sale for the premises was £525. He had a mortgage of £4OO and _h° sold the premises to pay off some of his creditors. Ili° wife claimed all furniture at 51, Tennyson-street, Liverpool (where she and the bankrupt now resided). she having purchased it from the. trustee acting under the deed of assignment with money given to her by her mother.—The examination was closed.</p>
      </div>
