      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Exam</head>
        <p>The Exam</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS:</p>
        <p>THE almost successful are the most unregarded of men, but by no means the unhappiest. MisTp,Ess : "Have you that ribbon for my hair ? " Maid: "Yes, mum; but—" "But what? "I've mislaid the hair !" DOCTOR: "Stick out your tongue, Tommy." Tommy: "Not me. I did that yesterday to my teacher, and I still ache all over for it." "Boy, how can I get to the railway station from here ? " "Have you got a carriage ? " "No, my little lad." "Oh, then you'll have to walk." "Miss ADA, would you—er—consider me bold if I were to—er—throw a kiss to you?" " Bold ! I should consider it the quintessence of laziness." PROFESSOR: "Can you name the bones of the skull? Student: "I've got them all in my head, professor, but I can't name them." SECRET. —" Oh, Edgar! it's delightful, this being bocretly engaged and nobody knowing any- thing about it. All my friends are envying me for it !" ACCOUNTING FOR IT.—" We don't heal. you sing nowadays, Miss Tomkins." My physician has strictly forbidden me to practice." Does he live in your neighbourhood ? " MOTHER: "My daughter, I'm astonished at your conduct with that gentleman. I distinctly heard him kiss you twice." Daughter: "Non- sense, ma. You know very Niel' the conservatory has an echo."</p>
        <p>LADY (meeting old servant): "Why, you left us, Jane, because we couldn't pay you higher wages, and now you say you're working for a master who pays you nothing for your labour !" Jane (sadly) : Yes, mum. I'm married, mum !" TEACHER: "What is a machine that is made to navigate the air commonly called?" Scholar: "A balloon." Teacher: "Correct. Now what is the man who navigates the balloon called?" Scholar: "A balloonatic." _ _</p>
        <p>"THOSE pugilists look very much alike," said Spatts. "Wait until they begin work, and you'll Lind the resemblance even more striking," replied Bloobumper." PASSENGER: "You don't go half as fast as you lid 20 years ago." Driver: "Well, I can't under- stand it; I've got the same horses now as I had then." _ _ _</p>
        <p>SHE: "Why aren't you married, Mr. Batch ? " He t‘ Oh, I'm too poor." She: "Poverty is no bar to marriage." He : "1 know it isn't, but it is considerable of a bar to its proper maintenance." "SORRY, Miss Spooner' but you're much too pretty for a nurse." "Sir !" "That's right, and you know it. I hired you to watch this sick young man, and, by Jove, the condition is just reversed."</p>
        <p>MlsTnEss "How is it, whenever I come into the kitchen, I find you gossiping with the baker or the butcher ? " Maid: "I should say it was them nasty soft-soled shoes you come creeping about in."</p>
        <p>"WILL you give me a kiss, Johnny?" asked a spinster of a five-year-old. "No, indeed," replied Johnny. "Why not?" she asked. "Cause if I did, the next thing you would be asking me to marry you," was the unexpected reply. ARTIST (angrily) : "Why did you fellows sky my Portrait of a Gentleman'? No one can see it without a step ladder." Hanging Committeeman: "Very sorry ; but, you see he looks like the sort of a person who would feel more comfortable up there than down on a level with ordinary people.' DICK: "You told me yesterday that you had proposed to Miss Coupon by mail. Did you get any letter in reply?" Harry: "Yes, I got two letters." Dick: Then it is all settled ? " Harry: "Yes. Yes, it is all settled. The letters were n' and `o."</p>
        <p>- TEACHER : " Samrcty, in the sentence, "I have a book, what is the case of the pronoun "I' ? Sammy (promptly): "Nominative case." Teacher: "Next boy, tell me in what case to put the noun book." Next boy (thoughtfully) : "Book- case."</p>
        <p>MIKE: "I hear that yez had a bad fall." Pat: "Oi did that same, I war on a scaffold whin the rope broke. I hung on to the rope an' thin dropped te tire hrnlzA me skull." Mike: "But whoy did yez lave go uf the rope?" Pat; "Sure, Oi lift go to moisten me hands.' GARDENER: "Caught you stealing my apples at last, have I? Now, who was the other boy?" Boy (sullenly) "Shan't tell yer." Gardener: "All right, it doesn't matter, I've got you ! " Boy (triumphantly) : "You wouldn't have got me if I'd run away like Dick Martin did." MRS. SOMERS : "What delicately constructed things these big steamships are !" Mr. Somers: "Why do you say that ? " Mrs. Somers: "Just think of the breaking of a screw disabling the whole ship." MASTER: "Where is the North Pole, George?" Pupil: "Don't know, sir." Master: "You don't know?" Pupil: "No, sir. If all the great explorers we've heard about couldn't find it, you can't expect me to know where it is." PARKER "One gets a fair conception of dis- tance when one stops a moment to consider that the sun is over 90 million miles from the earth." Sparker : "That's nothing. I called on a daughter last night that seemed a good deal more distant than that."</p>
        <p>YOUNG HUSBAND: "lam just about dead-beat putting down this carpet." Wife: "The carpet is not heavy." "No ; but I have to work in such a cramped position." " Nonsense ; just imagine you are on your bicycle." • " TOMMY, said a mother to her small son, "I'm afraid that when your father comes home all tired out with his day's work, and learns how naughty you have been, he will punish you." "Well,' replied the precocious youngster, 'I hope he will be so tired that it won't hurt very much." MRS. BE SCIENCE "If lam able to carry the baby I should think you ought to be." Professor Be Science: "You forget, my dear, that an average man has to carry an atmospheric pressure of 15 tons, while woman, being smaller, does not have to carry more than 10 or 12 tons." EXPLAINED AWAY.—First Lady: "I saw your husband meet you in the street yesterday, and I noticed that he removed his hat while speaking to you. I admired him for it. Very few men do that." Second Lady; "I remember. I told him in the morning to have his hair cut, and he was showing me that he had obeyed." • "MY dear Cohen, what has happened?" "Haven't you heard? I'm a bankrupt. I've lost everything I had in the world." "Dear, dear ! I did bear that you had lost a good deal of money, but it never occurred to me that it was your money."</p>
        <p>AN amusing incident occurred in one of the com- mon pleas courts the other day. The lawyer for the defence was making a very lengthy cross- 'examination of an old lady, when he was inter- rupted by the judge with the remark: "I think you have exhausted this witness." "Yes, judge," she exclaimed, "I do feel very much exhausted:"</p>
        <p>HER THEORY.—Mrs.. Mild "You seem to differ froth the usual idea about coddling a man to win his consent. According to your theory, scolding is the best medium for winning satisfactory results." Mrs.- Wild: "That my dear, is in accm d with one of the laws of Nature. gvery- thing has got to be blown up before it will come down."</p>
        <p>AN old Sussex farmer visiting London for the first time was taken into St. Paul's Cathedral. He stared about him in amazement, and his astonish- ment at the magnitude of the building seemed too great for utterance. It was only when he stood under the dome and gazed down the vast nave and up to tile dim and misty roof that his admiration at last found vent in words : " My ! what a lonia barn his 'ud make !" QUITE A SIMPLE MATTER. —Jilkins is the factotum of an artist who has made sonic noise in the world, and is quite as important a personage, in his own estimation, as his master. One (lay a lady called on the artist in the absence of the latter, and was received by Jilkins. " Dear me ," she cried, glancing first at an incomplete picture and then at Jilkins, " I declare it is very much like you." " It is me," said Jilkins with a superior air.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>.ner, Friday, March 13, 1903.</head>
        <p>.ner, Friday, March 13, 1903.</p>
        <p>CHAPMAN'S TRIAL. Southwark Poisoning eases. True Bill Returned.</p>
        <p>The March Sessions at the Old Bailey opened on Monday.</p>
        <p>The kecorder (Sir Forrest Fulton), in charging the grand jury, said the most serious charge in the calendar was that of 4nurder. The person accused was indicted in the name of George Chapman, a name which was thoroughly English, both as regards Christian name and surname. He was not, however, sorry to find that it transpired during the investigation of the charges that the mans name was not George Chapman at all, but either a Polish or a Russian Jew, having the name of Severino Rlosowski. This man seemed to have carried on in his own country an occupation similar to that which used to be called in England a barber surgeon. This condition of things still existed in Russia, or Poland, wherever this man came from, and therefore he had some sort:otknowledge of the action and use of drugs. Evidence would be given to show that the accused had made a purchas4 of a considerable quantity of tartar emetic, which contained antimony—a very deadly poison, and one which caused terrible agony to persons to whom it was administered. It had another peculiarity, that of preserving the corpse a much longer time than in the ordinary course of nature it would be preserved. Sir Forrest Fulton, dealing with the ques- tion,of motive, said it would seem that the prisoner, having gratified his desires with these women, wished to get rid of them. He thought they would have no difficulty whatever in returning a true bill. The grand jury returned a true bill against Chapman in each case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Retirement of Colonel Ireland Blackburne.</head>
        <p>Retirement of Colonel Ireland Blackburne.</p>
        <p>The retirement of another veteran Lancashire officer is announced, Colonel R. Ireland-Blackburne, C. 8., of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment (4th Royal Lancashire Militia) giving up the command, which he has held for over six years. He served during the recent embodiment in South Africa, and was mentioned in Lord Roberts' despatches. Colonel •Blackboume got his company in April 1875, and field rank in February 1892. He is the fifth of the eleven Lancashire Militia commanders who have re- tired since the early days of the late war. The colonel received the Order of the Bath for his South African services.</p>
      </div>
