      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, March 13, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 13, 1903.</p>
        <p>BRIGHTENING THE LIVES OF EISTEDDFOD AT EARLESTOWN. awarded the prize was received with almost frantic , applause from the audience. WORKHOUSE INMATES. i I Great credit is due to the Secretary, Mr. John ; Holland) and Committee for the success which attended the competitions, and there is no doubt but that the The Brabazon Scheme at Warrington. ' ffair has quite "taken on' in the district. The usual tzotes of thanks concluded the entertainments.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The Council Chamber at the Town Hall, Warrington, on Tuesday afternoon was utilized by the ladies con- nected with the Brabazon scheme, for the annual sale of articles made by the sick inmates of the Warrington Workhouse Infirmary. The room had been very taste- fully decorated for the occasion with choice flowers and plants, arranged by Mr. Parker, the Corporation head gardener. The articles included wool rugs, plain sew- ing, bent iron work, photograph frames, and several works of art by one of the inmates. There was a large attendance of ladies. The opening proceeding were presided over by the Rev. Father Ryan; of the Church of the Sacred Heart, Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>THE 'ANNUAL' REPORT. Miss C. BROADBENT (the hon. secretary) read the annual report, as follows felt very diffident about undertaking the post of Brabazon secretary, but I have had so many kind and willing helpers that the work has really been a great pleasure, and I think we have had a very good year. It is very satisfactory to see how many of the inmates have been cheered and brightened by their work. Cer- tainly, thejoy of produeing something is very great to them. We have had the most loyal and kind support from Mrs. Gate, the matron, and many valuable sugges- tions. We have a great deal of help, too, from the nurses, who make suggestions as to what kind of work is suitable for different individuals. We have some plain sewing this year, which is a new departure. We have a great many wool rugs, and as many more have been sold during the year, nearly all of them made by two men, Morris and Watson. I am very proud of my artist, a man named Grundy, whose work is really very original and pretty, though he never tried his hand at anything of the kind before. We have also a photograph frame made of bent iron work, by an invalid soldier in the town, whom we are allowed to bring within the scope of the society. He, too, has been immensely cheeredby the work, which makes him forget himself and his troubles. My list of Brabazon workers is as follows :—Miss Birtles, Miss Coxon, Mrs. John Davies, Mrs. Frost, Miss Nannie Fair- clough, Miss Gandy, Miss Henshall, Miss Jenny John- ston, Mrs. H. Lowe, Miss Bessie Pierpoint, Miss Ethel Roberts, Miss Belle Wright, and Miss Clarice Walker. It sounds rathera formidable list, but, some of them are unable to be regular, and, as a rule, we do not have more than six or eight present, so that the two hours allowed to us once a week soon go in supplying materials for work and saying a kind word to every one in the wards, which is what we try to do. We are very anxious, if possible, to make a little more than usual by the sale this year, as there are four old soldiers in the consumptive ward, and we want to send them to the seaside. I have had £2 ss. given me, but that Is only enough for one man for three weeks, and all would be immensely cheered, and, perhaps, some per- manently benefited by the change. We have a library in connection with the Brabazon Society, and it is run with the greatest possible energy and enthusiasm by Miss Ethel Roberts and Miss Jennie Johnston, who may be seen every Tuesday staggering under theirheavy burden of books. May I beg more books? We welcome any- thing that is good of its kind and wholesome reading— religious books, novels, history, travels. Wehave people of varied tastes, and it is very difficult to keep them supplied. There is one man who has taught himself Greek and is constantly reading and translating. We often supply spectacles out of the Brabazon fund, and they area great boon to many of the inmates. We want to thank the Mayor for lending us the use of the Town Hall, also Mr. Connor who has conveyed the work here free of charge; also Mr. Johnson who has kindly lent the tables; and above all let me thank and welcome Mrs' Peter Rylands, an old and valued friend, whom we are delighted to see amongst us once more, taking a warm Interest in all that concerns the good of the town." (Applause). Miss Broadbent read thebalance sheet for the year, showing a balance in hand of .£2 ss. 11d. AN APPRECIATION OF THE WORK.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN observed that he. felt convinced the society was doing a great work, not so much with re- gard to the money which was raised but in bringing delight and pleasures to those inthe hospital who could not help themselves.He had had a great deal of experience in hospital work at the Warrington Workhouselnfirmary during the past ten years, having visited it three or four times a week, and he could assure all present that before the Brabazon Scheme came into operation in Warrington there was a great void in the lives of the poor people at the Institution. There were inmates who were not bodily sick,who hadsomething the matter with a leg or an arm which kept them in bed all day. Before the scheme came into operation they had very little to keep their minds exercised, and they lay in bed like logs. Their minds were a torturA to them, but now they were cheered and looked forward with pleasure to the time when the ladies in connection with the Society, who took such a deep interest in them, visited the wards. (Applause.) It was verykind of the ladies to take such a deep interest in them, and it brightened their lives and made the time pass away pleasantly. The Chairman alluded with regret to the manner in which some soldiers were treated on their return from South Africa, India, and other places, and said it was a pity that after they had been fighting for the country and were invalided home they should have to go almost straight to the Workhouse. During the last two years he had seen several soldiers who had had to go to the Workhouse, and at the present moment there was one there who met with a serious accident while in the Transvaal. He was disabled and no one did anything for him. It was a pity that the responsible authorities did nothing for these men. The ladies con- nected with the Brabazon scheme were doing what they could to make the lives of the sick inmates brighter and he trusted they would be able to do much more in the future. (Hear, hear.) MRS. RYLANDS DECLARES THE SALE OPEN. Mrs. PETER RYLANDS who had a hearty reception, re- marked that after the excellent report which had been 'read by Miss BROADBENT and the remarks of the Chairman it was not necessary for her to say more than a few words. Neither was it necessary to say anything to commend such a society as the Brabazon Society to their sympathy. They could easily imagine what a boon it must be to the poor old people and invalids to have such a useful and interesting occupation put into their hands as it did so much to enliven the tedium and dul- ness of their lives. There was no doubt that unsuspected faculties had been developed, and. the wo&amp;taught and put into the fingers of the inmates meant their partial sup- port after leaving the Workhouse Infirmary. She was sure that the heartiest thanks of all were due to those ladies who week after week so kindly gave their time to teach- ing the different kinds of work to the inmates. it must be very uphill work and very discouraging, and needed the greatest patience, but they must feel amply repaid when they saw the useful and cleverly made articles spread about the table; showing that much brightness had been introduced into the lives of the poorest. She had very great pleasure in declaring the sale open, and hoped all the articles would be disposed of. (Applause.) The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Rylands for her services, and said she was so well known in the town for the good work she did that no words of commendation were needed for him. It was only to be expected that she would take a deep interest in the work done by such a society as the Brabazon Society, which was an excellent institution. The MAYORESS seconded the motion which was carried with great heartiness. Mrs. RYLANDS acknowledged the compliment and the sale was opened in earnest. A cafe chantant added considerably to the funds of the society.</p>
        <p>The Alleged Frauds by a Tailor. At Chester Assizes on Wednesday George Gittens, tailor, of Blackpool, surrended to his bail, and pleaded not guilty to three charges of obtaining sums of 3s 6d. by false pretences from Joe Martin, Disley, William Cowsill, Lymm, and Robert Peel, Altrincham. The prosecution said prisoner inserted an advertisement in Manchester newspapers, offering 500 pairs of trousers at 2s 6d. and 3s 6d. ea:th. After hearing part of the evidence Mr. Justice Bruce ruled that there was no case for the jury, and Gittens was at once discharged— Mr. Trevor Lloyd submitted that defendant's business was perfectly genuine, and it was shown by the evidence for the prosecution that he had a large stock of trousers for sale at the price advertised.</p>
        <p>?'Mr. Arthur R. Pennell, a Buffalo attorney, who was named by the murdered Mr. Burdick as co-respondent in his divorce suit, has met a terrible death through running his motor-car into a quarry 35ft. deep. The police suggest suicide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
