      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>Education Question,</head>
        <p>The Education Question,</p>
        <p>The adjourned meeting of the above Council to con- sider the Technical Instruction Committees' minutes was held on Wednesday evening, Mr. S. Taylor pre- siding. Present :—Messrs. W. Handley, D. Bisbrown, M. Sweetman,J.H. Salkeld, T. Jones, F. Wiswall, S. Walton, J. Wilson, J.I. Shaw, T. Dean, R.H. Posnett, E. Imison. J. Pritchard, F. Hewitt, with Messrs. F.A.Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (secretary), and W. Wilding (surveyor.) The Secretary read a letter from the County Council asking whether the Urban Council were prepared to levy a, rate under Section 3, of the Education Act, 1903, for education other than Voluntary in the district. The Council wished to know the amount of such rate in order to estimate what might be required from the County Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dean, in reply to the Chairman, asked whether they could, have the report of the gentlemen who attended Crewe.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson reported that the repiesentatives; duly attended the Council. Prior to the Conference the re- presentatives of the Urban Councils met to frame sug- gestions for submission to the County Council. Among those submitted was one by Mr. Handley which was adopted and which suggested that nothing should be done to prejudice the continuance with the Urban District Councils of any property vested in them. The following suggestions were adopted for submission to the County Council:- I.—That it is desirable that a Local Sub-Committee should be appointed for each Urban District, as provided for by Clause 6 of Schedule 1 of the Education Act, 1902,and also by Clause 3 of the 3rd Schedule. 2.—That such Sub-Committee should consist of repre- sentatives of the Urban Council, the Technical Instruc- tion Committee, the school board and voluntary schools, and representatives of grammar schools, in accordance with a scheme to be submitted to and approved by the County Council Education Committee, with power for Urban Districts to combine, but without prejudice to the continuance in the Urban Council of any property vested in the Urban Council. 3.—That such Sub-Committee should act under the control of the County Council Education Committee, and subject thereto should have the following powers. (1) Power to manage technical or higher schools(not grammar schools). (2) Power to manage evening schools. (3) Power to manage provided schools. (4) Power to carry out bye-laws and enforce atten- dance, and to direct attendance officer in the discharge of his duties.</p>
        <p>(5) Power to inspect all schools and report to the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>(6) Power to ap-p-o-intimpil teachers where more than one candidate.</p>
        <p>(7) Power to consent to appointment and dismissal of all teachers.</p>
        <p>All the representatives expressed the strong disinclina- tion to part with properties hitherto vested in the various Councils and declared their readiness to con- tinue to levy a penny rate for secondary education pur- poses with the stipulation that a very large measure of management be accorded to the various localities. The Urban Councils representatives With those of the County Boroughs were then very cordially received by the Chairman. Mr. Thomeycroft, of the County Council's Committee asked if they were satisfied with the treatment they had hitherto received at the hands of the County Council. On all sides satizfac- tion was expressed and he then declared that the County Council would go on the lines previously followed by them. A gentleman asked "If a rate of one penny be levied who would have the control or the spending of it ?"—The Chairman replied "The Council levying the rate and no one else."—The Clerk also stated that the Act did not take any property from the Urban Councils, and that it was doubtful which rate— the general district or the poor rate—the penny rate would have to come out of.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dean asked when would the County Council take over secondary education?</p>
        <p>Mr. Handley saia the County Council at the wish of the Board of Guardians would take over the powers as Goon as possible, not later than the Ist of May if possi- ble. The Board if necessary would Ante interim orders to put off the elementary part until September, the end of the financial year or half-year. It depended on the promulgation of the order which had not yet come to hand.</p>
        <p>The report having been received, Mr. Wilson moved that the Technical Instruction minutes be adopted as follows :—That under Section 3 of the Erbae.ti.. lona, 41-.. nau power to spend on secondary (including technical) education such a sum as shall not exceed the amount which wouldbe produoed by a rate of one penny in the pound. The Council was respon- sible for all liabilities in connection with the work carried on in the Technical Institute at the appointed day on which the Education Act would come into operation in this county. In addition to providing money for scholarship purposes and to meet the liabili- ties on the appointed day, the Committee were of opinion that it, was impossible to say what would be the position and requirements of the Institute during the coming year as to its income and expenditure. They therefore, recommended that a rate of one penny in the pound be levied for secondary education purposes. The Committee also reported that at present there were twelve students in the secondary day schools holding local scholarships; these expire at the close of the pre- sent year, and the donations required to continue them were not likely to be forthcoming. Scholarships of the following character are more desirable. There were four holders of local science and art scholarships in the school. It was recommended that these be increased to eight, and it would probably be found desirable to in- crease them to twelve a year hence. To comply with the conditions of the Board of Education the managers of the school must contribute £5 each year in respect of each scholar and lodge that sum annually with the Board before the 15th of April. For the first year of tenure of the scholarship, the local contribution would be supplemented by the Board with a grant of £4; for the second year with a grant of £7 ; and for the third year witha grant of £lO. Contributions from a rate, if assigned by a local authority for the purpose, meet the requirements of the Board. The committee therefore recommended that a sum of "40 be provided out of the id. rate for these scholarships. In moving the confirmation of the minutes Mr. Wilson said that the action of the Technical Instruction Com- mittee was the correct one. Until the new authority came into power the committee had to continue the work and provide for all contingencies. The Council would be the predominant partner in the new authority and the work would be carried on as before. The efforts for education had been recognised by the County Coun- cil who had given them year after year increased grants. They would work on the same lines as hitherto, and there was no vital principle at stake in levying the penny rate. Mr. Handley seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bisbrown : What will be the situation of the local authority with regard to the Technical schools ? The Chairman: I presume we shall go on as before. The Council will have power to co-operate on the com- mittee outsiders as before.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dean asked if they levied a penny. rate could they withhold any part of it at their pleasure? The Law Clerk: You have no right to use the rate for purposes other than it is levied for.</p>
        <p>4 The Secretary pointed out that the letter read was an annual letter sent to Urban Councils. If a rate of £.185 was levied and only JlOO was spent,the County Council I would cut the rate down accordingly. ! Mr. Posnett said the Council were being asked to show their hands by levying the rate and no practical • assurance had been given by the County Council that the Technical schools would be managed by themselves. He protested against the levying of the rate. Mr. Imison ridiculed Mr. Posnett's remarks and showed that for the contribution of the penny rats they were getting control for that amount. If the work of the Institute had to be carried on the rate must be levied. It must be levied to provide scholarships ex- clusively for their own district : to discharge the debt owittg and further because the County Council would I not discharge local obligations. I After further discussion the minutes were adopted I unanimously, Jr.A Posnett declining to vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>E COTTAGE HOSPITAL SCHEME.</head>
        <p>E COTTAGE HOSPITAL SCHEME.</p>
        <p>IMr. Posnett asked that the Council appoint a sub- committee of the General Purposes Committee to act with the Cottage Hospital Committee in furnishing the Cottage Hospital which would shortly be opened. Mr. Tmison contended that the thin end of the wedge was being introduced and that the Council would find itself landed in for financial liability if such a course were taken.</p>
        <p>After an informal debate it was resolved on the pro- position of Mr Wiswall that the Hospital Committee be appointed the Provisional Committee to carry on the business of the hospital until the deed was framed and the Council made the trustees.</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T. HOYE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above, on Monday -evening a splendid programme was given by several friends, arranged by Mr. G. Clarke, who very ably presided over a good company. The following programme was sub- mitted. Opening Ode, "Let the lower lights be burn- ing"; prayer, ode, "Temperance Bells are ringing"; solo "The little flower girl," Miss M. Stronach; recita- tion "Rose'y Ned," Mr. IV. Wilson ; solo "Betwee n Decks," Mr. W. Wheatley; violin solo "Cambria," Mr. Edmunds; solo "Cathedral memories," Miss E. Woods; duett "The pilot brave," Messrs. Twemlow and Wheatley; Lancashire reading, Mr. .1. Warburton; violin solo "Bolero," Mr. Edmunds; solo "Love i not for a day," Mr. Twenflow; dialogue "13rownand White,' Messrs. W. Wilson and G. Clarke. Miss Toombs was .accompanist. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the friends and a very enjoyable meeting closed by singing "God be with you till we meet again."</p>
        <p>MONEY WELL SPENT if you buy ALF TAYLOR'S good Boots, 45, Horsemarket-st.,Warrington.—Advt. UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>BA.SSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.---J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 7;5, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
