      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugbi—Football.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugbi—Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The fact that Oldham were engaged on Saturday in knocking Bradford out of the Cup Competition de- prived Warrington of a League fixture. The specta- tors at Wilderspool had, therefore, to be satisfied with second-rate fare in the shape of a match in the Lan- cashire Combination between Oldham "A," and Warrington "A." There was an excellent attendance nevertheless and I should think there were fully 3,000 spectators round the rails. The "A" team have, as is well-known, done remarkably well of late, and are in the running for the Combination medals. Last Saturday's match had an important bearing on the championship for Swinton were first, Oldham second, and Warrington third,and the victory of the locals by a goal to nil enabled them to beat Oldham for second place by a single point.</p>
        <p>After a very wet start the afternoon cleared up and there was an excellent game. Warrington, with Fish Burgess, Boardman and Morrison on the team ap- peared to have a fairly easy thing, but the Oldhamers soon knocked that notion on the head. The Water- sheddings Contingent included such class players as Vivian Huzzey, Sam Williams, J. Lawton, Civil, and Bonser, and the tearn altogether was remarkably strong. Forward they were superior to the homesters and the game resolved itself into a duel between the respective backs, which ended with honours even. The match would have ended in a draw but for Fish's goal, the result of an excellently judged penalty kick.</p>
        <p>Play was always interesting, and on the whole I should be inclined to dub Oldham the better side. Their combination was superior to that of Warring- ton,and had Fish not been on hand to frequently break up the movements they would probably have scored. Fish played a fine (lame, one run he made in particular being worth all the rest of the play put together. Burgess shaped badly, though he tried hard, and just now he is anything but a success at the base of the scrummage. Hispartner, Wilkinson, was the better of the two. on Saturday. In the three-quarter line Brooks was excellent on the defence, while Bailey and Watkins did smart work at times. For Oldham J. Lawton,at half-back, and Turner Wood at full-back, were the best men, though the forwards to a man played a grand game. Huzzey, Williams and Civil, were but a shadow of their former selves.</p>
        <p>The cup-ties on Saturday resulted peculiarly, but satisfactorily from a Lancashire point of view. One of the peculiar results was the defeat of the thrice- champions, Batley, whowent under at Barrow. They have only themselves to blame, for by playing against the wind from choice in the first half they allowed Barrow to get a lead sufficient to win the game. Halifax could only draw with Brigh.ouse, but they triumphed in the re-play on Tuesday. Salford and Runcorn won easily, and the astonishing result of the round is that there are now four clubs on each side of the border, notwithstanding the big advantage accru- ing to Yorkshire from the territorial system of drawing.</p>
        <p>The draw for the fourth round places long °clods on the cup remaining in Lancashire (or Cheshire) for the Palatinate representatives are all drawn at home with the exception of Salford, and all play Yorkshire clubs. It is very doubtful whether a representative of the broad-acred shire will exist after the 21st. The draw is as follows : Runcorn v. Halifax. Barrow v. Hull. York v. Salford. Oldham V. Hunslet.</p>
        <p>These should all provide good games, but as I have said, the odds are against the Tykes. .</p>
        <p>There were a. few Northern League matches on Saturday, and by defeating Wigan, Swinton placed themselves in the running for the championship once Helens= ...,orrb kr%LOl" fine performance in defeating the Mather Laners by 8 points to 5 points. This quite upsets Leigh's chances in the West Lancashire Competition and likewise makes it probable that they will be lower in the Northern League than Warrington at the end of the season.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows : For Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Halifax 26...17... 7...2...23 29-133...15 15— 75...36 Swinton 26...14... 5...7...34 49-215...17 17— 85...35 Salford 26...15... 7...4...24 40-168...19 16— 86...34 Runcorn 24...14... 6...4...27 39-171...15 14— 72...32 Bro'ght'n R 20...13... 8...5...23 38-160...14 15— 73...31 Bradford 26...13...10...3...2'7 36-162...21 25-117...29 Oldham 24...14...10...0...20 37-151...17 16— 82...28 Batley 25...12...10...3...27 27-135...26 25-127...27 Hull 26...12...12...2...15 33-129...23 27-127...26 Hunslet 25...12...11...2...36 21-135...28 40-176...26 Leigh 26...10...11...5...25 19-107...19 26-116...25 Warrington 24... 9...11...4...16 18— 86...22 28-128...22 Wigan 24... 8...12...4...20 18— 94...23 27-127...20 Huddersfield 26... 9...15...2...11 21— 83...27 32-150...20 Widnes 25... 9...15...1...19 17-, 89...19 30-128...19 Hull K. R. 24... 8...14...2...11 19— 79...29 36-166...18 Brighouse R 26... 6...17...3...15 11— 63...28 36-164...15 St Helens 26... 6...19...1...23 13— 85...31 60-242...13 The South-West Lancashire League table stands as follows :--</p>
