      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>Leigh „..</head>
        <p>Leigh „..</p>
        <p>9 ... 3 ... 3 ... 3 ... 43-- 40 ... 9</p>
        <p>Wigan Widnes 7 -. 3 -. 4 -. 0 -. 21-- 22 _. 6 St. Helens 7 -. 1 -. 6 -. 1 ._ 19-- 75 -. 3</p>
        <p>My readers will be pleased to learn that the emissaries of the club have succeeded in signing on a promising centre three-quarter in the person of Richard Mahoney of Cardiff, a young player with a good reputation in South Wales. Mahoney is of, medium size, standing 5 feet 8 inches high, and weighing 10 stones 12 lbs.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington entertain the Rangers of Broughton, with whom they drew at Wheater's Field, and have every hope of beating. They certainly ought to do so. Most clubs have taken the Rangers down recently, and Warrington should certainly do likewise on the Wilderspool patch. The Rangers have not much chance for the Championship but they may be relied upon to play a hard game, and they have selected the following Strong fifteen :—Fielding: Hogg, Wilson, Barrett, and Kirkham ; James (S) and James (W) ; Oram, Ruddick, Woodhead, Winskill, Scott, Beetham, Thompson and Whitehead. Reserve: Preston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>From Mr. E. Pickavance (chairman), I have received a copy of the annual report and handbook of the Liver pool centre, N.C.U. It is a tastefnlly got-up produc- tion, consisting of 56 pages, and full of information interesting to all cyclists in this neighbourhood whether members of the N.C.U. or not. The Liver- pool Centre offers numerous advantages to riders to join, and I wish it well in its efforts on behalf of the great army of wheehnen. There are not many refer- ences to Warrington in the booklet, but in the Roads Committee's Report I notice that "the tram-lines in Bridge-street, Warrington, being above the level of the road, this matter was brought before the Borough Surveyor, Mr. Longdin, and promptly • received his attention." This note refers to the Rylands-street corner, where there was originally a very , dangerous ridge of setts along the curve of the lines. Two local clubs, the Warrington Cycling Club and the Warring- ton Royal Cycling Club are members of the Liverpool Centre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>Angling.</head>
        <p>Angling.</p>
        <p>At the last meeting of the Committee of the War- rington Anglers'Association, eighteen new members, in addition to several juveniles, were enrolled. The number returned to the Provincial Angling Associa- tion for travelling cards is 600. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Fred J. Emson assistant secretary on his successes in the compilation of the Angler's Year Book. It was announced that the length of the river Gowy opposite the lace Hall Estate is now rented by the Ellesmere Port and District Angling Society. The Victoria Angling Society have fixed upon Saturday, July 25th for their annual con- test. The Workingmen's Angling Society enrolled nine new members on Monday evening last. The King Edward VII. Angling Society have decided to fish their contests on August 16th and September 20th. The Latchford Excelsior Angling Society are offer- ing four prizes for a competition for speci- men :eel in addition to their other "setts." Fishing at Llangollen on Monday last several local rods recorded good sport., Mr. J. Briars, captured a trout of 11b. 130 z., and a dace of 9oz. ldr., Mr. Joseph Bailey, a roach of some 11-lbs., and a daCe of 9ozs., Mr. T. Cooper, a roach of 111bs., and Mr.</p>
        <p>_4</p>
        <p>William Hamblett some roach of nice size. Several other anglers managed to creel a trout or two. Some other local rods who visited Chester, Rossett, Walcot and Shrewsbury last week end report little sport. Mr. F. J. Emson will read a pape: on "The Natural History of a Fish" to the members of the Latchford Angling Society on Monday evening next. The open season for course fish expires to-day Saturday.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Baptist Church, Golborne-street. The Church was well filled on Sunday evening, when the Pastor dealt in his address with questions received concerning " The Atonement," the subject of previous discourses. By various illustrations it was shewn that Christ's atonement for sin was not only necessary, but absolutely essential and indispensable to fallen man.</p>
        <p>The Rev. B. Percival's Return. On Sunday evening there was a large congregation at St. Peter's church when the Rev. B. Percival, vicar, who last week reiturned home from Switzerland, where he had spent several months for the benefit of his health, occupied the pulpit. The rev. gentleman expressed his thankfulness that he had been so restored to health as to enable him to resume his duties in the parish. He paid a high compliment to his curate and church officers, and thanked the congregation generally for their many kindnesses to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-13_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was an average attendance at the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society held on Sunday afternoon in Wycliffe Church. This was the second Sunday of the , eleventh year's existence of the Society, and 28 new members were enrolled, bringing up the membership to 426. Mr. Pickavance presided, and in the course of an interesting address referred to some of the men of the past he had known who . had been identified with good work. Although they had gone the work still remained to be carried on and he appealed to young men to take up the work with zeal and earnestness. Mr. E. C. Pearson, M.A., son of the Rev. S. Pearson, of Broughton Park, Manchester, delivered an address on "Personal Influence." They must first have this influence, he said, then use it, and use it well. They were all able to exercise some influ- ence, but they must see to it that their influence was for good and not for bad. Two solos were sung by Mr. Jones, one of them being from Sankey's hymns book and the other" A Dream of Paradise." The service was also taken part in by Mr. F. Brown and Mr. C. Cotton. Councillor Dodds at Buckley-street. Advice to Young Men. The half-yearly distribution of prizes in connect lot with Buckley-street P.S.A., tookplace in the Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street, on Sunday. There was a large attendance of members of the society, which, it is interesting to note, is for men and women and boasts of an increase of 50 new members this half-year. Mrs.and Miss Dodds distributed the prizes, which numberedlso. The chair was taken by Councillor Dodds. Mr. Tom Cook read the lesson: "Come unto Me" and "Light in darkness" were effectively rendered by Mrs. Park- inson. and Mr. J. Greenough was appreciated in "Star of Bethlehem" and "The Watches." Mr, Albert E. Trow was the accompanist. Councillor DODDS, in the course of his address, said it was highly satisfactory to him to feel that he had the confidence and support of the religious people in the public work he had undertaken. He had always lived a quiet life, and would be glad to get back again to private life' but now he had put thearmour on he was not going to take it off until he had seen the business through. He thanked the members of that P.S.A. personally for the confidence they had placed in him that day. He asked the young men to be diligent in life. Theirs was the golden age of life—often it was called the seed-time. If they sowed tares instead of wheat they would practically reap nothing, but if they were diligent in doing good they would be successful in life. He advised them to read that which would do them good. They must consider what book they really wanted, and then get it. it was a sad thing to think that Warrington had been held back from real education by sectarianism, but the new education authority would do all it possibly could to advance the education of the children. He bade them, as a society, "Be of good cheer, and tryst in God." At the conclusion of the distribution of prizes, the PRESIDENT (Mr. J. J. Fairhurst), on behalf af th A.,. presented Mr. 1-1,1.1. isn.k, entitled i "Noble thoughts n noble languages." Mr. Dodds had always been willing to help them and they greatly appreciated his services. They hoped the book wool d prove useful to him, and trusted he would accept it as a token of their regard for him. Councillor DODDS' in reply, expressed his thanks for the kind expressions of their President, and for thliik book which the society had presented to him. It ha come as a surprise to him, and he would value it very much. It had always been a pleasure to him to help ia the work of the Master.</p>
