        <p>ATARE I NGTON UN lON.—PERSONS willing CONTRACT for the supply of any of the under- mentioned articles front Lady Day to Michaelmas 'next to be delivered free of expense at tbe Warrington Union Work- house, or at the Indusi,rial Sehools, Padgate, at such times and in such quantities as the Board of Guardians shall direct are requested to send in DIN DIMS properly sealediendorsed and free of expense,addressed to the Clerk of the Guardians, before four o'clock p.m. on TUESDAY, the 24th day of MARCH. 1903. The following are the articles for which TENDERS are required : Groceries and Provisions Shoes, Clogs and Slippers Beef and Mutton Glazing Joints for Officers Brushes Flour and Irish pin head meal Coffins Loaves and Bread Best Peninaenmawr Stone Straw and Potatoes for breaking purposes Drapery Ac.. Leather Cord and Twill Coal, Slack, and Gas Coke. Samples of any of the above may be seen on application to the Master of the Workhouse. The Coal and Slack supplied must be to the satisfaction of the House and Schools Com- mittees. The Pit and quality of coal must be named in the, Tender. Forms of tender and full particulars as to the prob- able, quantities of each article required may be obtained at the Clerk's Office. Each tender must be made upon the prescribed form and properly enclosed and endorsed with the name of the article tendered for. Each article tendered for must be of 81 cc material and quslitu shown at the Workhouse. No tenders for other articles will be accepted. Bach contractor must be prepared (if required) to enter into security for the due per- formance of his contract. The Guardians do not bind themselves to alcept the lowest or any tender. All con -tract accounts will be paid quarterlv. —By order. JAMES CLO% ES SUTTON, Clerk to the Guardians. Bewsev Chambers, Warrington, February 26th, 1930.</p>
